const BQ=[
{"id":"V1","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A market-intelligence service uses a coordinator agent that dispatches six research subagents in parallel for each client question. Reviewers report that final briefings often cover the same region three times while missing others entirely. Traces show the coordinator's task messages to three subagents all read \"research market trends in Asia-Pacific\", with no other instructions.\n\nWhich change most directly fixes the problem?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Have the coordinator give each subagent a distinct scope, sources, output format and exclusions, and check coverage before synthesis"},{"l":"B","t":"Move the subagents to a larger model tier so each one notices which regions the others cover"},{"l":"C","t":"Let the subagents post their progress to a shared channel so they can negotiate which regions each of them should research"},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the number of parallel subagents from six to ten so that every region is more likely to be covered by at least one of them"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Subagents start with only the task message the coordinator writes. When three of them receive the same one-line brief, duplicated work and silent gaps are the expected result. The coordinator must divide the work explicitly (scope, boundaries, output format) and reconcile coverage before it synthesizes. Larger models, more subagents and peer chatter do not fix an underspecified delegation.","x":["Correct. Overlap and gaps come from vague delegation, so explicit boundaries and a coverage check close both.","Incorrect. Subagents run in isolated contexts and cannot see each other's work, whatever their size.","Incorrect. Peer negotiation adds coordination overhead and new failure modes; the coordinator owns the division of work.","Incorrect. More subagents with the same vague brief multiply the overlap and the cost without guaranteeing coverage."],"df":"medium","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V3","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"To fix production bugs, a team built a three-agent pipeline: a reproducer writes a failing test, a debugger finds the cause, and a fixer writes the patch. Each agent receives a summary from the previous one. Post-incident review shows the fixer often patches a symptom, because details the debugger ruled out never reached it.\n\nWhat is the most effective change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make each handoff summary longer so that the debugger passes every hypothesis it considered on to the fixer agent"},{"l":"B","t":"Give the fixer a larger model tier so it can infer the cause from the failing test"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep reproduce, debug and fix in one agent context, delegating only isolated lookups to subagents"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a fourth agent that reviews each handoff summary and asks the previous agent for anything that it left out"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Sequential agent pipelines work when the stages are independent. Bug fixing is the classic counterexample: every stage depends on discoveries from the previous one, and each handoff compresses them. Keeping the dependent work in one context, and delegating only self-contained lookups, removes the lossy boundaries instead of decorating them.","x":["Incorrect. Longer summaries still lose detail at every boundary and add tokens to each handoff.","Incorrect. A larger model cannot recover evidence it was never given.","Correct. Each step depends on what the previous one learned, so those steps belong in one context.","Incorrect. Another handoff adds latency and a new place for information to be lost."],"df":"hard","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V4","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A coding agent delegates test runs to a \"test-runner\" subagent that replies \"12 passed, 3 failed\". The main agent then writes throwaway debug scripts to rediscover why the three tests failed, and runs take 40% longer than before the subagent was added.\n\nWhat should the team change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Split the test runner into two parallel subagents, one for unit and one for integration tests"},{"l":"B","t":"Run the tests in the main thread so the full failure output stays where the fix is written"},{"l":"C","t":"Give the test-runner subagent a stronger system prompt asking it to explain the cause of every failure it reports"},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the main agent's effort level so that it reasons harder about the three failing test names it receives"}],"a":["B"],"r":"A subagent is worth its overhead when the intermediate work does not matter to the main thread. Test output is exactly what the fixing context needs, so summarizing it forces rediscovery. The decision rule is whether the intermediate work matters: if it does, keep it in the main thread.","x":["Incorrect. Splitting the runner hides the failure output in two places instead of one.","Correct. The main thread needs the failure details, so hiding them behind a summary costs more than it saves.","Incorrect. The subagent's explanation is still a lossy summary of output the fixing context needs.","Incorrect. More reasoning over three names cannot replace the stack traces it never saw."],"df":"medium","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V6","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A research coordinator decides at run time how many subagents to start. After a prompt change, the median run on simple factual questions starts 30 subagents and costs nine times more, while answer quality is unchanged.\n\nWhich change best contains this failure?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each subagent in its prompt to finish faster and use fewer tool calls"},{"l":"B","t":"Disable parallel dispatch so that the coordinator starts subagents one at a time and can stop as soon as it has enough"},{"l":"C","t":"Switch the subagents to the smallest model tier so that the extra subagents cost less for every run that starts them"},{"l":"D","t":"Cap subagents and tokens per run in the harness, and tell the coordinator what each question type warrants"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Over-spawning is a coordinator scaling failure. Two controls work together: guidance on effort scaling (simple questions need one or two subagents) and a hard budget in the harness so that no prompt regression can multiply cost without limit. Cheaper or slower subagents treat the symptom.","x":["Incorrect. Asking subagents to hurry does not stop the coordinator from starting too many.","Incorrect. Serial dispatch slows complex questions and still lets the coordinator over-spawn.","Incorrect. Cheaper subagents still multiply cost without improving the answers.","Correct. A harness cap bounds the damage and the guidance restores sensible scaling."],"df":"hard","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V7","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A procurement assistant has one agent that browses supplier websites and also holds a tool that writes purchase orders to the ERP system. A red team shows that text hidden on a supplier page can make the agent raise an order.\n\nWhich TWO changes create an effective context-isolation boundary? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Merge every tool into one larger agent so that a single model sees the whole task and can judge each action"},{"l":"B","t":"Give every agent the full conversation so that each one can spot injected instructions that the others miss"},{"l":"C","t":"Move web reading to a subagent with read-only browsing tools and no ERP access"},{"l":"D","t":"Tell the agent in its system prompt to ignore instructions found on supplier websites"},{"l":"E","t":"Have that subagent return validated fields, such as price and lead time, not raw page text"}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"The exploit needs untrusted content and a consequential tool in the same context. Splitting them across a boundary, with a read-only subagent for the untrusted pages and a narrow structured interface back to the agent that acts, removes the path. Prompt instructions and shared context leave it open.","x":["Incorrect. One agent with every tool is exactly the configuration the red team exploited.","Incorrect. Sharing context spreads the untrusted text to the agent that holds the write tool.","Correct. The context that reads untrusted pages can no longer reach the action that matters.","Incorrect. Instructions alone are not a boundary; the exploit path stays open.","Correct. Structured fields keep injected instructions out of the agent that can write orders."],"df":"hard","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V8","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"On Claude Managed Agents, an incident-response session uses a coordinator and three specialist agents that share one file system. Specialists sometimes overwrite each other's findings in /work/notes.md, and the coordinator's summary then omits whole lines of investigation.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run the three specialists one after another so that only one of them writes to the shared notes file at a time"},{"l":"B","t":"Let the coordinator rewrite the notes file after each specialist finishes, keeping whatever looks most complete to it"},{"l":"C","t":"Give each specialist its own output file under /work/findings/ and have the coordinator merge them"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the specialists in their prompts to append to the notes file, never overwrite it"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Parallel agents that share storage need separate write targets or a coordination mechanism. Giving each specialist its own output path keeps the work parallel and lets the coordinator merge from complete inputs. Serializing works but discards the benefit, and prompt requests do not prevent races.","x":["Incorrect. Serial runs avoid the conflict but give up the parallelism that justified specialists.","Incorrect. The overwritten findings are already lost by the time the coordinator reads the file.","Correct. Separate output paths remove the write conflict while keeping the shared file system.","Incorrect. A prompt request does not make concurrent writes to one file safe."],"df":"medium","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V14","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An agent fleet runs unattended overnight to reconcile vendor invoices. Payments above a set limit require a finance approver. Approval requests raised at 2 a.m. currently block the whole job until morning, so invoices that need no approval also miss the payment run.\n\nWhat is the best redesign?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Pause only that invoice, notify the approver, continue the rest, and decline if no answer arrives in time"},{"l":"B","t":"Move the job to business hours so an approver is always available"},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the approval requirement for overnight runs and have finance review those payments the following morning"},{"l":"D","t":"Let the agent approve payments above the limit when its own confidence score in the invoice is high enough"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Human-in-the-loop gates should block only the action that needs a decision. Unattended runs also need a notification path and a safe default when no one responds, which for payments means declining. Removing the gate or delegating approval to the model breaks the control.","x":["Correct. The gate stops only what it must, and a timed default keeps the run predictable.","Incorrect. The job loses the overnight window the payment run depends on.","Incorrect. Reviewing after payment defeats the purpose of a pre-payment approval.","Incorrect. A model's confidence is not an authorization control for money movement."],"df":"medium","ob":"Multi-Agent Orchestration"},
{"id":"V19","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team self-hosts the Agent SDK in short-lived containers to run customer onboarding agents that pause for days while waiting for documents. Resuming a paused onboarding sometimes fails because the session cannot be found.\n\nWhich TWO changes make resumption reliable? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the agent's max turns so that the whole onboarding finishes in one session without pausing"},{"l":"B","t":"Store session transcripts on a persistent volume or external store rather than the container's local disk"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep every container running for the whole multi-day wait so that its local session files are never lost"},{"l":"D","t":"Start a fresh session on resume and paste the customer's uploaded documents into the first message"},{"l":"E","t":"Record each customer's session ID alongside the onboarding record, and resume by that ID"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Agent SDK sessions are persisted where the host stores them. In ephemeral containers that storage must be external or mounted, and the application must keep the session ID with its own business record so it can resume the right session. Keeping containers alive for days is costly and fragile.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting for a customer cannot be shortened by giving the agent more turns.","Correct. Local session files disappear with the container, so resumption needs durable storage.","Incorrect. Idle containers for days cost money and still die on host maintenance.","Incorrect. A fresh session loses the decisions and context of the earlier conversation.","Correct. A stored session ID lets the harness reattach the right conversation days later."],"df":"medium","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V20","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An engineer asks Claude Code to refactor a module. Midway through, Claude runs a shell script that rewrites generated files and also edits three source files with its edit tool. The engineer rewinds to the checkpoint before the refactor and finds the generated files still changed.\n\nWhy did this happen?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rewind restores only the conversation by default, and code must always be restored with a separate git command"},{"l":"B","t":"Checkpoints expire after compaction, so any edits made before the last compaction can no longer be rewound"},{"l":"C","t":"Checkpoints track Claude's file edits, not changes made by shell commands, which need version control to undo"},{"l":"D","t":"Generated files are excluded from checkpoints by the .gitignore file, so Claude Code never snapshots them"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Claude Code's checkpoints capture changes made through its file-editing tools. Changes made by bash commands, external processes or other sessions are not tracked, so durable recovery for those still depends on version control. Long sessions that run scripts should commit at safe points.","x":["Incorrect. Rewind can restore code, conversation or both; the gap is specific to shell changes.","Incorrect. Compaction does not explain why only the script's changes survived.","Correct. Rewind restores edits made through Claude's edit tools; shell side effects are outside it.","Incorrect. The issue is how the files were changed, not whether they are ignored."],"df":"medium","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V21","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A long-running coding agent works through a 200-item feature list over several sessions, each starting with a fresh context window. Later sessions sometimes redo finished features or declare the whole project done after a few items.\n\nWhich TWO practices best address this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run the entire 200-item list in one session with compaction so that no new context ever has to orient itself"},{"l":"B","t":"Let each session decide for itself which features are done by reading the code and judging its completeness"},{"l":"C","t":"Have each session read a progress log and the recent commit history before choosing the next item to work on"},{"l":"D","t":"Keep a structured feature list in a file, with a pass or fail status per item, updated only after tests pass"},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the agent in its prompt to be careful not to declare the project finished before every item is done"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Multi-session agents need durable, explicit state: a structured list of work items with status changed only when verification passes, plus a progress log and commits that each new session reads first. Without those artifacts, every fresh context guesses, which produces both repeated work and premature completion.","x":["Incorrect. Compaction drops detail, and the run still has to survive interruptions.","Incorrect. Judging completeness from code is exactly how features get redone or skipped.","Correct. Reading durable progress artifacts orients a fresh context quickly and accurately.","Correct. A machine-readable status list tells each new session what is truly finished.","Incorrect. A reminder does not give the new context the facts it needs."],"df":"hard","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V22","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An agent that books freight shipments runs as a series of steps: quote, reserve, pay, confirm. A crash after the payment step but before confirmation leads the restarted run to pay again for 0.3% of shipments.\n\nWhat is the most robust fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Restart from the start and tell the agent to check for a payment"},{"l":"B","t":"Move the payment to the last step so that a crash before it never leaves a paid but unconfirmed shipment"},{"l":"C","t":"Retry the confirmation step more aggressively so that runs crash less often between payment and confirmation"},{"l":"D","t":"Journal completed steps, and send an idempotency key from the shipment with the payment"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Resumability requires knowing which steps completed and making any repeated side effect safe. A step journal lets the restarted run skip work already done, and an idempotency key built from the business action makes the payment system return the original result if the call is repeated.","x":["Incorrect. The earlier messages are gone or unreliable after a crash.","Incorrect. Reordering changes which step is exposed but leaves a crash window around payment.","Incorrect. Fewer crashes in the window still leave duplicate payments when they happen.","Correct. The journal skips finished steps, and the key makes a repeated payment call harmless."],"df":"medium","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V27","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An agent on the Messages API reaches the end of a long tool-use turn, and a response comes back with stop_reason pause_turn while running the web search server tool. The harness currently treats this as a finished answer, so users receive partial results.\n\nHow should the harness handle it?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Send the response's content back as an assistant message with the same tools so Claude continues the turn"},{"l":"B","t":"Treat pause_turn as an error and fall back to a second model that does not use server tools at all"},{"l":"C","t":"Retry the original request from scratch with a higher max_tokens value so that the turn cannot pause"},{"l":"D","t":"Return the partial answer to the user and ask whether they want the agent to search the web again"}],"a":["A"],"r":"The API can pause a long-running turn that uses server tools and return pause_turn. The harness continues it by sending the response content back as the assistant turn, with the same tools, in the next request. Treating it as final or as an error loses work.","x":["Correct. A paused turn resumes when its content is returned in a new request.","Incorrect. Falling back discards the progress the paused turn already made.","Incorrect. pause_turn is not a token-limit stop, so a bigger budget does not prevent it.","Incorrect. The turn is incomplete, and asking the user hands them the harness's job."],"df":"medium","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V29","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"An agent that reviews insurance claims pauses when it needs a supervisor's approval. In Claude Managed Agents, the client sees the session go idle and assumes the work is complete, so approvals are never collected.\n\nWhat should the client check?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The session's title, since the agent renames the session when it needs a human to approve something"},{"l":"B","t":"The environment's networking mode, since limited networking makes approvals impossible to collect"},{"l":"C","t":"The number of agent messages in the session, since a finished session always has more than one"},{"l":"D","t":"The idle event's stop reason, since requires_action means a tool call is waiting for an answer"}],"a":["D"],"r":"session.status_idle means the agent has stopped and is waiting for input. The stop reason distinguishes end_turn (finished or interrupted) from requires_action (a tool call needs an answer, such as an approval). Treating every idle event as completion drops pending approvals.","x":["Incorrect. Titles are set by the client and do not signal state.","Incorrect. Networking does not change how approval requests are signaled.","Incorrect. Message counts say nothing about why the session stopped.","Correct. Idle can mean finished or waiting, and the stop reason says which."],"df":"easy","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V30","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A legal-research agent saves checkpoints every 30 seconds by serializing its full conversation, including every tool result, to a database. Checkpoint writes now take 40% of run time, and restoring a checkpoint into a new run exceeds the context window.\n\nWhich TWO changes improve the checkpoint design? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep bulky tool results in external storage and checkpoint references to them, loading them again only when needed"},{"l":"B","t":"Checkpoint more often so that each write holds fewer new messages and finishes faster than before"},{"l":"C","t":"Stop checkpointing and rely on retries"},{"l":"D","t":"Compress the serialized transcript before writing it so that the database stores fewer bytes per checkpoint"},{"l":"E","t":"Checkpoint compact state at step boundaries, not the full transcript"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"A checkpoint should hold what a new run needs to resume, not everything the old run saw. Step-boundary checkpoints of compact state, with large artifacts stored externally and referenced, keep writes cheap and restores within the context window. Compressing or writing the transcript more often does not fix the design.","x":["Correct. References keep checkpoints small while preserving access to the evidence.","Incorrect. More frequent writes of the full transcript make the overhead worse.","Incorrect. Without checkpoints, every interruption restarts hours of work.","Incorrect. Compression shrinks storage but the restored transcript still overflows the context.","Correct. Compact step-level state is fast to write and fits back into a new context."],"df":"hard","ob":"Durable Execution and State Management"},
{"id":"V35","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A document-intake pipeline is a fixed chain: classify, extract, validate against a schema, write to the records system. The team wants to replace it with an autonomous agent because \"agents are the future\". In production the chain is 99.1% accurate at a stable cost per document.\n\nWhat is the best decision?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the workflow, since the evidence shows fixed steps that it handles accurately at predictable cost"},{"l":"B","t":"Replace the chain with a multi-agent system, one agent per step, so that each step can be improved separately"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the chain but let Claude decide at run time whether to skip the validation step on easy documents"},{"l":"D","t":"Replace the chain with an agent and run it in shadow mode, switching over once it matches 99.1% accuracy"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Architecture changes should be justified by cost, reliability or quality data. A fixed chain that is accurate and predictable is the right pattern for steps that can be listed in advance. Agentic designs earn their cost where the path varies, which is not the case here.","x":["Correct. An agent adds variance and cost to steps that are already known and reliable.","Incorrect. Splitting a working chain into agents adds handoffs and coordination failures.","Incorrect. Letting the model skip validation removes the control that keeps accuracy high.","Incorrect. Even a matching agent would cost more for no gain on known steps."],"df":"medium","ob":"Architecture Selection at Scale"},
{"id":"V36","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team is comparing two designs for a contract-review service in production: a fixed workflow and an agent that decides which clauses to examine. They will run both on live traffic for two weeks before choosing.\n\nWhich TWO measures should drive the decision? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"How often each design's output contains the phrase \"no issues found\" in the summary"},{"l":"B","t":"The average number of tool calls each design makes per contract during the comparison"},{"l":"C","t":"Cost per correctly reviewed contract, including retries and human corrections"},{"l":"D","t":"Total tokens consumed per day by each design across all contract types combined"},{"l":"E","t":"The rate of contracts escalated to a lawyer or corrected after review, by contract type"}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Comparing architectures in production needs outcome measures: what each correct result costs, and where each design fails by segment. Activity counts such as tool calls or raw token totals do not capture quality, and surface text features do not measure correctness.","x":["Incorrect. A phrase count says nothing about review accuracy.","Incorrect. Tool call counts measure activity, not whether contracts are reviewed well.","Correct. Cost per successful outcome captures both spend and quality in one figure.","Incorrect. Daily tokens ignore quality and mix differences between the two designs.","Correct. Escalation and correction rates by type show where each design falls short."],"df":"medium","ob":"Architecture Selection at Scale"},
{"id":"V39","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team plans to standardize on a third-party agent framework for all new services. During a pilot, engineers could not see the exact prompts sent to Claude, and a framework upgrade silently changed how tool results were formatted.\n\nWhich TWO requirements should the team set before adopting any framework as a standard? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The framework hides prompt construction so that teams cannot introduce inconsistent prompts"},{"l":"B","t":"The framework supports every model provider so that teams can switch providers on every request"},{"l":"C","t":"Framework upgrades are pinned and pass the team's evaluation suite before rollout"},{"l":"D","t":"Engineers can see and log the exact requests sent to Claude, including tool results"},{"l":"E","t":"The framework always uses the largest available model so that results are as good as possible"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Frameworks can speed development but can also obscure the prompts and responses underneath, which makes debugging and evaluation harder. A standard should require visibility into the exact requests and controlled, evaluated upgrades. Abstraction that hides prompts is a reason for caution, not a feature.","x":["Incorrect. Hiding prompts is the problem the pilot exposed.","Incorrect. Provider breadth does not address visibility or upgrade safety.","Correct. Pinned, evaluated upgrades stop silent behavior changes reaching production.","Correct. Teams cannot debug or evaluate prompts they cannot see.","Incorrect. Model choice is a per-route decision, not a framework rule."],"df":"hard","ob":"Architecture Selection at Scale"},
{"id":"V41","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An organization has 30 teams building on Claude. Each team writes its own system prompt for the same company-wide procedures, such as how to cite internal policy and how to format incident summaries, and the procedures drift between teams.\n\nWhich TWO reusable-pattern standards fit this problem? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each team to copy the best-performing team's system prompt into its own repository once a quarter"},{"l":"B","t":"Package each shared procedure as a Skill with a named owner, versioned and loaded only when a task needs it"},{"l":"C","t":"Let each team keep its own version"},{"l":"D","t":"Merge every team's prompt into one shared system prompt that every application loads for every request"},{"l":"E","t":"Distribute approved Skills through a central registry"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Procedures that must be applied consistently across many teams belong in reusable, owned packages, such as Skills distributed through a central registry or marketplace. Copying prompts or loading one monolithic prompt either drifts or wastes context.","x":["Incorrect. Copies drift again the moment either version changes.","Correct. Skills keep one governed copy of a procedure that loads on demand.","Incorrect. Drift in company-wide procedures is the problem, not a sign of fit.","Incorrect. One giant prompt loads irrelevant procedures into every request.","Correct. Central distribution keeps every team on the reviewed version."],"df":"medium","ob":"Architecture Selection at Scale"},
{"id":"V43","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A logistics company runs a Claude agent on Amazon Bedrock. The team wants to adopt a Claude API feature that is in beta, and the Bedrock deployment is required by the company's cloud agreement for all production traffic.\n\nWhich TWO steps reflect sound architecture selection? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"If it is not, design for what Bedrock supports now and plan to adopt the feature later"},{"l":"B","t":"Move production traffic to the first-party API"},{"l":"C","t":"Assume every beta feature is available on all platforms at the same time and build on it now"},{"l":"D","t":"Check whether the feature is available on Bedrock before designing around it"},{"l":"E","t":"Ask Bedrock support to enable the beta feature for the company's account ahead of its public release"}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Cloud platforms and the first-party API do not always have the same features at the same time. When a platform is mandated, architecture decisions must be checked against that platform's current capabilities, with a path to adopt newer features once they arrive there.","x":["Correct. The cloud agreement is the binding constraint, so the design fits the platform.","Incorrect. The scenario says the agreement covers all production traffic.","Incorrect. Parity is not guaranteed, so assuming it risks a design that cannot ship.","Correct. Features can reach the first-party API before cloud platforms, so availability must be confirmed.","Incorrect. Hoping for early access is not a design plan."],"df":"hard","ob":"Architecture Selection at Scale"},
{"id":"V46","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A log-investigation agent calls a search tool that returns up to 8,000 tokens per call. After about 40 calls, requests approach the context limit and cost per run has tripled, although the agent only ever uses the last few results. Its instructions and early decisions are still needed throughout.\n\nWhich context policy fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use compaction on every turn so that the whole conversation is summarized before each new search call"},{"l":"B","t":"Raise max_tokens on each request so that the agent has more room left over for its search results"},{"l":"C","t":"Use context editing to clear older tool results automatically while keeping recent ones and the conversation"},{"l":"D","t":"Move the instructions into the first user message so that the system prompt takes less room in the window"}],"a":["C"],"r":"The evidence says old tool results are the problem and the conversation itself is still needed. Context editing that clears older tool results, keeping the most recent ones, targets exactly that. Compaction rewrites everything, which is heavier and lossier than needed here.","x":["Incorrect. Summarizing every turn is costly and can drop the early decisions the agent needs.","Incorrect. max_tokens limits output, not the input that is filling up.","Correct. Stale tool output is the bulk of the context, and clearing it keeps decisions intact.","Incorrect. The instructions are small; the tool results are what fill the window."],"df":"hard","ob":"Context and Memory Architecture"},
{"id":"V48","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A customer-support conversation is cached up to the end of the system prompt. When a customer upgrades to a premium plan mid-conversation, the application edits the system prompt to add premium instructions. Cache hit rate on those conversations drops to near zero for the rest of the session.\n\nWhat is the cache-safe way to apply the change?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Edit the system prompt as now, but mark the edited section with a second cache breakpoint"},{"l":"B","t":"Start a new conversation with the premium system prompt and replay the earlier messages into it"},{"l":"C","t":"Move the premium instructions into the tool definitions, which sit outside the cached prefix"},{"l":"D","t":"Leave the system prompt unchanged and add the new instruction as a message later in the conversation"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Prompt caching matches an exact prefix: tools, then system, then messages. Editing the system prompt changes the prefix and invalidates everything cached after it. A mid-conversation change of instructions is cache-safe when it is appended later in the message history instead.","x":["Incorrect. The edit still changes the prefix, so everything after it misses the cache.","Incorrect. Replaying the history writes a new cache entry at full cost.","Incorrect. Tool definitions are part of the prefix, so editing them also breaks the cache.","Correct. Content after the cached prefix can change without invalidating it."],"df":"hard","ob":"Context and Memory Architecture"},
{"id":"V49","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An agent that drafts long technical reports uses server-side compaction. Engineers notice that after compaction the agent forgets a formatting requirement the user stated early on, and twice it repeated a section it had already written.\n\nWhich TWO changes best preserve what matters across compaction? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Turn compaction off and let requests fail when the context fills so that no detail is ever summarized away"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep durable requirements and progress in a file or memory store that the agent reads after compaction"},{"l":"C","t":"Give compaction instructions on what the summary must keep, such as user requirements and sections written"},{"l":"D","t":"Restate every user requirement in the system prompt after each turn by editing the prompt dynamically"},{"l":"E","t":"Trigger compaction more often so that each summary covers fewer turns and is less likely to lose details"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Compaction is a summary and can drop details. Two complementary controls help: tell the summary what to keep, and store durable requirements and progress outside the conversation where the agent can read them back. Disabling compaction or rewriting the system prompt each turn creates bigger problems.","x":["Incorrect. Failing requests is worse than a summary that keeps the key facts.","Correct. Requirements stored outside the conversation survive any summary.","Correct. Steering the summary keeps the details that matter for the task.","Incorrect. Editing the system prompt each turn breaks caching and still misses new decisions.","Incorrect. More frequent summaries compound losses rather than prevent them."],"df":"hard","ob":"Context and Memory Architecture"},
{"id":"V53","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A legal assistant must answer questions about a 900-page regulatory code. The team first loaded the entire code into every request, which took 600,000 tokens and made answers slower and less accurate on specific sections.\n\nWhich TWO designs follow just-in-time context? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A lookup tool that returns a section by its identifier when Claude needs it"},{"l":"B","t":"A memory file that holds the full code so that the agent reads it at the start of each session"},{"l":"C","t":"A cached system prompt that holds the entire code so that later requests cost much less"},{"l":"D","t":"A search tool over the code that returns the few most relevant passages with section references"},{"l":"E","t":"A compaction step that summarizes the regulatory code before the first question is asked"}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Just-in-time context loads what the agent needs now and lets tools fetch the rest. A section lookup tool and a passage search keep the window focused on relevant text. Caching, summarizing or reloading the whole code keeps the window crowded or loses precision.","x":["Correct. Sections arrive only when a question needs them.","Incorrect. Reading the whole code from memory is the same overload by another route.","Incorrect. Caching cuts cost but keeps 600,000 tokens of mostly irrelevant text in context.","Correct. Retrieval brings in the relevant passages instead of the whole code.","Incorrect. A summary of the code loses the exact wording legal answers depend on."],"df":"medium","ob":"Context and Memory Architecture"},
{"id":"V58","s":1,"d":1,"ty":"single","q":"A product manager asks why the team's agent gets slower and more expensive the longer a conversation runs, even though each user message is short.\n\nWhat is the best explanation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Caching expires after the first turn"},{"l":"B","t":"Each request re-sends the whole conversation and tool results"},{"l":"C","t":"Short user messages are padded to a minimum length by the API, which adds cost on every turn"},{"l":"D","t":"The model slows down over time because it gets tired after many turns in the same conversation"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Every turn sends the entire context again: system prompt, tools, history and tool results. That is why late turns cost more and take longer, and why context policies such as clearing, compaction and memory matter for long-running agents.","x":["Incorrect. Caching makes repeated prefixes cheaper; it does not cause the growth.","Correct. The full context is input on every call, so cost and latency rise with history.","Incorrect. The API does not pad messages.","Incorrect. Models do not tire; the growth comes from the input size."],"df":"easy","ob":"Context and Memory Architecture"},
{"id":"V61","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A document-summary service retries every failed Claude request up to five times with exponential backoff. Logs for one week show that 30% of retries are for responses with stop_reason max_tokens, and every one of those retries is truncated at the same point as the original.\n\nWhat is the right handling for these responses?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep retrying them with longer backoff, since a later attempt may produce a shorter summary by chance"},{"l":"B","t":"Treat them as successful responses and return the truncated summary to the user without changes"},{"l":"C","t":"Route them to a fallback model with a larger context window and the same max_tokens setting"},{"l":"D","t":"Raise the output budget or continue from the partial response, instead of resending the same request"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A max_tokens stop means the response hit the output budget. Resending the identical request reproduces the truncation and wastes tokens. The correct branch raises the budget, asks for a shorter output, or continues from the partial response.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting does not change the output budget that caused the stop.","Incorrect. Returning a cut-off summary hides the failure from the user.","Incorrect. The limit hit is the output budget, not the context window.","Correct. A length stop repeats exactly when the same request is resent."],"df":"medium","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V62","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A customer-facing assistant falls back to a second model on any failure. After a model update, traces show three stop conditions mixed in the fallback path: 429 rate-limit errors, responses that stopped at max_tokens, and responses with stop_reason refusal. Costs have risen 35%.\n\nWhich TWO changes improve reliability? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Route all traffic to the fallback model permanently, since it answers the retried requests"},{"l":"B","t":"Send refusals to a safety-handling path instead of retrying them on another model"},{"l":"C","t":"Remove the fallback entirely and return the first error to the caller for every failure"},{"l":"D","t":"Retry 429 errors after the retry-after interval with jittered backoff, on the same model"},{"l":"E","t":"Raise the retry count for every failure type so that more requests eventually succeed"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"These are different failure classes with different correct responses: back off and retry rate limits, handle length stops by changing the budget, and handle refusals on a safety path. A single undifferentiated retry path is the root cause of the cost spike.","x":["Incorrect. A permanent switch trades one problem for another without classifying failures.","Correct. Retrying a refusal elsewhere repeats it at extra cost and may be inappropriate.","Incorrect. Removing the fallback loses the resilience it gives for real outages.","Correct. Rate limits clear with time, so waiting is the right response.","Incorrect. More retries of the wrong kind multiply the cost spike."],"df":"hard","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V70","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team's retry layer has no global limit. In a partial outage, each user request can produce up to 5 retries, and each agent turn may make 6 model calls, so load on Claude rose to many times normal and made recovery slower.\n\nWhich TWO controls bound the retry load? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Doubling the number of retries per request so that more user requests succeed during an outage"},{"l":"B","t":"Removing backoff so that retries complete sooner and free their connections more quickly"},{"l":"C","t":"A retry budget that caps retries as a share of total requests across the service"},{"l":"D","t":"Retrying at one layer only, rather than at the client, the agent loop and the gateway at once"},{"l":"E","t":"Retrying each failed call on all fallback models in parallel to raise the chance one succeeds"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Retries multiply: retries per call times calls per request times layers that retry. A service-wide retry budget and a single retrying layer keep total load bounded during partial outages. More, faster or parallel retries make recovery slower.","x":["Incorrect. More retries per request deepen the overload.","Incorrect. Immediate retries concentrate load at the worst moment.","Correct. A budget stops retries from multiplying load during an outage.","Correct. Retries at several layers multiply into each other.","Incorrect. Parallel retries across models multiply load further."],"df":"hard","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V72","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A team adds cross-region fallback for its Bedrock deployment. In a failover test, the secondary region returns the same model, but responses fail validation because the team's prompt-caching configuration and one tool version differ between the regions' supported features.\n\nWhat does this test show?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Fallback paths must be tested for configuration and feature parity before they are relied on"},{"l":"B","t":"The secondary region is misconfigured by AWS and will start working once the provider fixes it"},{"l":"C","t":"Cross-region fallback is never useful, so the team should rely on retries in the primary region"},{"l":"D","t":"The team should remove prompt caching from all requests so that every region behaves the same way"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Fallbacks across regions, models or platforms are only as good as their last test. Supported features and versions can differ, so failover paths need their own configuration and regular exercise. A test that fails is the cheapest place to find that out.","x":["Correct. The test exposed parity gaps that would have broken a real failover.","Incorrect. Feature availability differs by region and version; it is not a fault to wait out.","Incorrect. A tested fallback still protects against regional outages.","Incorrect. Dropping a cost control everywhere is a heavy price for an untested fallback."],"df":"hard","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V74","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"Rate-limit responses from the Claude API include a retry-after header. A team's client ignores it and retries with a fixed one-second delay, and during peaks most retries are rejected again.\n\nWhat change makes the client's retries effective?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Retry immediately and repeatedly so that the client gets back into the queue before other clients do"},{"l":"B","t":"Lower max_tokens on retried requests so that the API is more likely to accept them during the peak"},{"l":"C","t":"Wait at least the retry-after interval before retrying, adding jitter so clients do not retry in step"},{"l":"D","t":"Switch to another API key whenever a 429 arrives so that requests draw on a different rate limit"}],"a":["C"],"r":"When the API sends retry-after, the client should honor it and add jitter so many clients do not retry at the same moment. Immediate retries and key rotation make peaks worse or break the usage terms.","x":["Incorrect. Immediate retries are rejected and add to the peak.","Incorrect. The limit is on request and token rate, and smaller outputs do not reset it.","Correct. The header says when capacity will be available, and jitter spreads the retries out.","Incorrect. Rotating keys to evade limits is not a legitimate throughput strategy."],"df":"medium","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V76","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A summarization service sets max_tokens to 64,000 for long reports and uses non-streaming requests. Some requests fail with network timeouts in the team's HTTP client even though the API would have completed them.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Retry timed-out requests on a fallback model that generates text faster than the primary"},{"l":"B","t":"Split every report into 16 parallel requests and join the outputs in order afterward"},{"l":"C","t":"Lower max_tokens to 4,000 for all reports so that every request completes quickly enough"},{"l":"D","t":"Use streaming for long generations, so the connection stays active while output is produced"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Long non-streaming requests can exceed client or network idle timeouts before the response arrives. Streaming keeps data flowing while the model generates, which is the recommended approach for long outputs.","x":["Incorrect. A faster model may still time out and changes quality.","Incorrect. Splitting a coherent report into independent parts damages it.","Incorrect. A low cap truncates the long reports the service exists to produce.","Correct. Streaming avoids idle-connection timeouts on long responses."],"df":"medium","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V77","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team is writing a runbook that tells its retry layer how to treat each Claude API outcome.\n\nWhich TWO entries are correct? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"stop_reason refusal: retry the same request three times, since refusals are often transient errors"},{"l":"B","t":"400 invalid_request_error: send the same request to the fallback model, which may accept it"},{"l":"C","t":"401 authentication_error: retry with backoff, since credentials often become valid again within minutes"},{"l":"D","t":"413 request_too_large: do not retry as is; reduce the request size before sending it again"},{"l":"E","t":"529 overloaded_error: retry a small number of times with backoff, then use the tested fallback"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Reliability runbooks classify outcomes. Overloads are transient and suit bounded retries plus fallback; oversized requests must be reduced first. Authentication errors, invalid requests and refusals are not fixed by retrying the same request.","x":["Incorrect. A refusal is a model decision, not a transient error.","Incorrect. An invalid request is invalid on any model.","Incorrect. Authentication failures need a credential fix, not retries.","Correct. The same oversized request will be rejected every time.","Correct. Overload is transient, and a fallback covers it if it persists."],"df":"medium","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V78","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A payments company's fraud-review agent calls Claude and then an internal case-management API. During a network partition, the agent's Claude calls succeed but case updates fail, and the agent tells analysts the cases were updated.\n\nWhat is the most important reliability fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Remove the case-management tool and have analysts update cases from the agent's summary by hand"},{"l":"B","t":"Retry the Claude calls more aggressively"},{"l":"C","t":"Report the case-update failure instead of success"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a fallback model so that a different model reports the outcome of the case update to analysts"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Reliability includes truthful outcomes. A tool failure must flow back as an error so the agent reports it, retries safely or escalates. Here the integration treated a failed side effect as done, which is worse than an outage because no one knows to act.","x":["Incorrect. Removing the integration solves nothing about honest error reporting.","Incorrect. Claude calls were not the part that failed.","Correct. The agent must not report success for an action that did not happen.","Incorrect. Another model would report the same wrong outcome from the same inputs."],"df":"hard","ob":"Reliability Engineering for Claude Services"},
{"id":"V80","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A SaaS platform serves 400 business tenants from one Claude organization. One tenant's bulk import job used most of the shared rate limit for two hours, and interactive requests from other tenants were rejected.\n\nWhich TWO controls give tenant isolation? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Asking tenants in the documentation to spread their bulk jobs across the day to avoid peaks"},{"l":"B","t":"Per-tenant quotas enforced at the platform's gateway, such as token buckets for requests and tokens"},{"l":"C","t":"Separate workspaces with their own rate and spend limits for workloads that must not compete"},{"l":"D","t":"A larger organization rate limit so that the bulk tenant cannot reach it during its imports"},{"l":"E","t":"A single first-come, first-served queue for all tenants so that every request waits its turn fairly"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Multi-tenant isolation needs enforcement: per-tenant quotas at the gateway, and separate workspaces with their own limits where workloads must be kept apart. A shared queue or a bigger shared limit leaves the noisy-neighbor problem in place.","x":["Incorrect. Guidance does not enforce isolation.","Correct. Each tenant's usage is capped before it can consume the shared limit.","Correct. Workspace limits keep a heavy workload from starving others.","Incorrect. Any shared limit can be exhausted by one heavy tenant.","Incorrect. One shared queue lets a bulk job fill it ahead of interactive users."],"df":"medium","ob":"Scale and Throughput Management"},
{"id":"V82","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An insurer's quote assistant has an interactive chat and a nightly job that re-scores 200,000 policies. Both share one rate limit. When the nightly job overran into business hours, chat users received 429 errors.\n\nWhich TWO changes protect interactive traffic? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run the re-scoring job through the Message Batches API instead of the real-time API"},{"l":"B","t":"Retry chat requests more aggressively so that they win the contention against the nightly job"},{"l":"C","t":"Give interactive requests priority in the gateway queue and shed or delay background requests first"},{"l":"D","t":"Lower the chat's max_tokens during business hours so that chat requests use fewer output tokens"},{"l":"E","t":"Move the chat to a smaller model so that chat requests fit in the capacity left by the job"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Two levers separate background and interactive load: move background work to batch processing, and prioritize interactive requests when contention happens anyway. Retrying harder or degrading the interactive experience treats the victim instead of the cause.","x":["Correct. Batch work is processed separately from real-time request limits.","Incorrect. Aggressive retries add load and make 429s more common.","Correct. Priority protects the traffic that has a user waiting.","Incorrect. Smaller chat answers do not stop the job from using the limit.","Incorrect. Changing chat quality to make room for a background job is backwards."],"df":"hard","ob":"Scale and Throughput Management"},
{"id":"V85","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"An analytics team runs a weekly evaluation of 50,000 prompts against two models to decide on a model change. The run currently uses the real-time API and takes nine hours because of rate limits.\n\nWhat is the most cost-effective approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reduce the evaluation to 500 prompts so that the run fits in the real-time limits within an hour"},{"l":"B","t":"Submit the evaluation prompts as message batches, since no one needs each result immediately"},{"l":"C","t":"Run the evaluation only on weekends when interactive traffic is low and limits are less contested"},{"l":"D","t":"Split the run across several API keys in the same organization so that it finishes faster"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Large evaluation runs are a strong fit for batch processing: they tolerate latency, cost less per token in batch, and do not compete with real-time traffic. Cutting the sample or juggling keys either weakens the result or does not help.","x":["Incorrect. Shrinking the set weakens the decision the evaluation supports.","Correct. Evaluation runs are offline work that batch pricing and limits suit.","Incorrect. Timing does not change the price, and results still take hours.","Incorrect. Keys in one organization share its limits."],"df":"medium","ob":"Scale and Throughput Management"},
{"id":"V89","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"An agent platform sees rising latency and occasional 429 errors. A breakdown shows the agents' average request now carries 120,000 input tokens because conversation histories grew after a feature change, while request counts are flat.\n\nWhich constraint is now binding, and what is the right response?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Requests per minute; reduce the number of agent turns by merging several tool calls into each turn"},{"l":"B","t":"Concurrent connections; open fewer HTTP connections by sending requests through a single client"},{"l":"C","t":"Output tokens per minute; shorten the agents' final answers so that each response uses fewer tokens"},{"l":"D","t":"Input-token throughput; cut the context per request with clearing, compaction or caching"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Identify the binding constraint from the data. Flat request counts with growing input per request point at input-token throughput. Context management and caching cut the tokens that count toward the limit; changes aimed at requests or output miss it.","x":["Incorrect. Request counts are flat, so they are not the binding limit.","Incorrect. Connection counts are not the constraint the data points to.","Incorrect. Nothing in the breakdown shows output growing.","Correct. The growth is in tokens per request, so the fix is less input per request."],"df":"hard","ob":"Scale and Throughput Management"},
{"id":"V95","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A team's batch job submits 120,000 requests nightly through the Message Batches API. Some nights a fraction of requests in the results are marked errored or expired, and the downstream report silently omits them.\n\nWhat should the pipeline do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Switch the job to real-time requests, which never fail or expire the way batch requests can"},{"l":"B","t":"Treat the batch as successful when its processing status is ended, since that means every request succeeded"},{"l":"C","t":"Check each result's status, resubmit errored or expired requests, and report any still missing"},{"l":"D","t":"Resubmit the entire batch whenever any request fails, so that all results come from the same run"}],"a":["C"],"r":"A batch ending does not mean every request succeeded. Each result carries its own outcome, so the pipeline must inspect them, resubmit errored or expired requests, and report anything still missing. Silent omission corrupts the downstream report.","x":["Incorrect. Real-time requests also fail and need handling.","Incorrect. Ended means processing stopped, not that every request succeeded.","Correct. Batch results are per request, so failures must be handled one by one.","Incorrect. Resubmitting succeeded requests wastes cost and time."],"df":"hard","ob":"Scale and Throughput Management"},
{"id":"V97","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A company exposes an internal HR MCP server remotely so that agents in several products can use it. The current design passes each user's corporate access token straight through from the client to the HR system's API.\n\nWhich TWO practices should the remote server adopt instead? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Put the HR API's service account password in each client's configuration so that calls are simpler"},{"l":"B","t":"Authorize clients with OAuth and validate that each token was issued for this MCP server"},{"l":"C","t":"Have the server obtain its own credentials for the HR API rather than forwarding the client's token"},{"l":"D","t":"Disable authentication on the MCP server, relying on the corporate network firewall to limit access"},{"l":"E","t":"Accept any valid corporate token that the client presents, since all employees already have one"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"MCP's authorization guidance says remote servers should authenticate clients with OAuth, validate that tokens were issued for them, and never pass client tokens through to downstream APIs. Token passthrough breaks audience checks and creates confused-deputy risks.","x":["Incorrect. Spreading a shared secret to clients multiplies the exposure.","Correct. The server accepts only tokens meant for it, which blocks reuse of other tokens.","Correct. Token passthrough lets any token holder reach the HR API through the server.","Incorrect. Network location is not an identity control for a remote server.","Incorrect. Accepting any token ignores its audience and invites confused-deputy attacks."],"df":"hard","ob":"Production MCP and Change Management"},
{"id":"V102","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A company routes all Claude traffic from its applications and from Claude Code through an internal LLM gateway that adds authentication, quotas and logging. After a gateway upgrade, requests that use a beta feature fail although the same requests work when sent directly.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The beta feature has been withdrawn by Anthropic on the same day as the gateway upgrade"},{"l":"B","t":"The gateway's quotas are rejecting beta requests because they use more tokens than others"},{"l":"C","t":"Claude Code cannot use an LLM gateway, so the requests must always go directly to the API"},{"l":"D","t":"The gateway no longer forwards the required headers, such as anthropic-beta, to the API"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A gateway sits between clients and the API, so it must preserve the request as the API expects it, including version and beta headers. When direct requests succeed and gateway requests fail after an upgrade, the gateway's handling of the request is the first suspect.","x":["Incorrect. The direct requests still work, so the feature exists.","Incorrect. A quota rejection would return a quota error, not a feature failure.","Incorrect. Claude Code supports routing through a gateway.","Correct. Gateways must pass the headers the API needs for the features clients use."],"df":"hard","ob":"Production MCP and Change Management"},
{"id":"V103","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A platform team operates MCP servers that 40 agents use. They want to change the output format of a search tool, and they cannot coordinate a simultaneous update of all agents.\n\nWhich TWO approaches let them change the tool safely? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Track which agents still call the old version and contact their owners before removing it"},{"l":"B","t":"Hide the change behind a random 50% split so that each agent sees both formats at random"},{"l":"C","t":"Publish the new format as a new tool version while the old one keeps working for a stated period"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask every agent owner to update on the same day and roll back the change if anyone is not ready"},{"l":"E","t":"Change the format in place and rely on the agents to adapt, since Claude reads any output format"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Shared tools need versioning and deprecation like any other shared API: run versions side by side, measure who still uses the old one, and retire it once callers have moved. In-place changes break callers, and synchronized cutovers rarely work across many teams.","x":["Correct. Usage data shows when it is safe to retire the old version.","Incorrect. Random formats make agent behavior unpredictable.","Correct. Old and new versions coexist while agents migrate at their own pace.","Incorrect. One late team blocks everyone, and rollback undoes the others' work.","Incorrect. Agents with code that parses the output will break."],"df":"medium","ob":"Production MCP and Change Management"},
{"id":"V104","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A team deploys its agent on both the Claude API and Amazon Bedrock. A prompt change is deployed to the Claude API path but forgotten on the Bedrock path, and for a week the two paths behave differently.\n\nWhat practice prevents this configuration drift?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Generate both platforms' configurations from one versioned source, with checks that they match"},{"l":"B","t":"Assign one engineer per platform to keep its configuration up to date by hand after each change"},{"l":"C","t":"Use only the Bedrock path from now on so that there is one configuration to maintain"},{"l":"D","t":"Review both configurations once a quarter and correct any differences that have built up"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Platform-specific configuration parity is a change-management problem. Deriving each platform's configuration from one versioned source, with automated checks for differences, keeps prompt and feature settings aligned across deployment paths.","x":["Correct. One source and a parity check keep the paths from drifting.","Incorrect. Manual updates by different people are exactly what drifted.","Incorrect. Dropping a platform may break requirements that put it there.","Incorrect. A quarterly review leaves weeks of divergence."],"df":"medium","ob":"Production MCP and Change Management"},
{"id":"V111","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A company's agents use Claude through both the first-party API and Microsoft Foundry. The team wants one deployment pipeline for prompt and tool changes.\n\nWhich TWO elements should the pipeline include? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Running the evaluation suite on each platform before promotion, since behavior can differ"},{"l":"B","t":"Per-platform settings beside one shared prompt and tool source"},{"l":"C","t":"A manual copy of the prompts to Foundry"},{"l":"D","t":"Deployment to the first-party API only, assuming Foundry stays in step automatically"},{"l":"E","t":"Identical model identifier strings on both platforms, since every platform uses the same names"}],"a":["A","B"],"r":"Multi-platform delivery works best from one source of prompts and tools with explicit per-platform settings, and with evaluation on each platform, since identifiers and supported features differ.","x":["Correct. Each platform's deployment is tested where it will run.","Correct. Shared content with per-platform settings keeps each deployment valid.","Incorrect. Manual copies are how platforms drift apart.","Incorrect. Nothing keeps the second platform in step without the pipeline doing it.","Incorrect. Platforms can use their own model identifiers."],"df":"medium","ob":"Production MCP and Change Management"},
{"id":"V114","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An electronics distributor's support assistant often fails on questions that contain exact part numbers such as \"TX-4410-B\", returning passages about similar parts. Questions in plain language work well.\n\nWhich TWO changes address this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Increase the embedding model's dimensions so that part numbers are represented more precisely"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove part numbers from the indexed text so that embeddings focus on the descriptions"},{"l":"C","t":"Ask users to describe the part in words instead of typing its number"},{"l":"D","t":"Filter or boost by structured part-number metadata when a query contains a part number"},{"l":"E","t":"Add keyword search, such as BM25, alongside embeddings and merge the two result lists"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Dense embeddings capture meaning but blur exact tokens such as identifiers and codes. Hybrid search adds lexical matching, and structured metadata filters handle exact identifiers directly. Both keep the strengths of semantic search for plain-language questions.","x":["Incorrect. Larger vectors still treat similar part numbers as near neighbors.","Incorrect. Removing identifiers makes exact lookups impossible.","Incorrect. Users naturally ask by part number.","Correct. Exact metadata matching finds the specific part directly.","Correct. Exact identifiers match on keywords where embeddings blur them."],"df":"medium","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V116","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A developer-tools company is choosing how its assistant should answer questions about a large, fast-changing monorepo. One option is a vector index of code chunks refreshed nightly; the other is agentic search with tools that list, grep and open files.\n\nWhich TWO factors favor agentic search here? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The code changes many times a day, so a nightly index is often out of date"},{"l":"B","t":"Vector indexes cannot store source code, so they are not an option for this corpus"},{"l":"C","t":"Agentic search is always cheaper per question than retrieving chunks from a vector index"},{"l":"D","t":"Agentic search has lower latency than vector retrieval for every kind of question"},{"l":"E","t":"Answers often require following references across files, which needs several targeted lookups"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Agentic search trades extra latency and tokens for freshness and the ability to follow references across files, which suits codebases that change constantly. RAG suits large, slow-changing corpora where a fast single retrieval step is enough.","x":["Correct. Live search reads the current code instead of a stale snapshot.","Incorrect. Code can be embedded and indexed; that is not the constraint.","Incorrect. Agentic search usually costs more tokens and time per question.","Incorrect. Several tool calls are generally slower than one index query.","Correct. Multi-hop questions suit an agent that decides what to read next."],"df":"hard","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V118","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A pharmacy chain's assistant answers questions about drug interactions from an internal knowledge base. Reviewers found answers that sounded certain even when the knowledge base had no relevant article.\n\nWhich TWO grounding practices address this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the temperature for more varied certainty"},{"l":"B","t":"Have Claude say when the sources do not cover the question"},{"l":"C","t":"When no article passes a relevance threshold, give a set response and route it to a pharmacist"},{"l":"D","t":"Remove the knowledge base and let Claude answer from its training, which covers common interactions"},{"l":"E","t":"Increase the number of retrieved articles so that some article is always included in the context"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Grounded applications must handle \"no evidence\" explicitly: instructions that permit and require \"I don't know\" when sources are silent, and a designed path when retrieval returns nothing relevant. Padding the context or relying on training data produces confident unsupported answers.","x":["Incorrect. Variation in tone is not a grounding control.","Correct. Declared uncertainty stops confident answers with no source behind them.","Correct. A no-evidence path is handled by design instead of improvised.","Incorrect. Ungrounded answers about drug interactions are the risk.","Incorrect. Irrelevant articles do not support an answer."],"df":"medium","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V120","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A support search retrieves the top 20 passages by vector similarity and passes the top 5 to Claude. An evaluation shows the right passage is in the top 20 for 92% of questions but in the top 5 for only 61%.\n\nWhich TWO changes are best supported by this evidence? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Add a reranking step that reorders the top candidates by relevance before choosing which to pass"},{"l":"B","t":"Measure recall at 5 and at 20 on the evaluation set after each change, to confirm the gap closes"},{"l":"C","t":"Replace vector search with keyword search, since embeddings are ranking the passages poorly"},{"l":"D","t":"Re-chunk the whole corpus into smaller pieces, since retrieval is missing most of the relevant passages"},{"l":"E","t":"Have Claude rewrite every answer twice and keep the version that mentions more of the passages"}],"a":["A","B"],"r":"The gap between recall at 20 and recall at 5 is a ranking problem. A reranker that scores candidates against the query moves the right passage into the top 5, and measuring recall at both cutoffs confirms the fix. Recall itself is already strong.","x":["Correct. The evidence is usually retrieved but ranked too low.","Correct. Stage-level metrics show whether ranking improved.","Incorrect. Swapping methods discards a recall that is already good.","Incorrect. Retrieval already finds the passage 92% of the time.","Incorrect. Rewriting answers does not change which passages reach Claude."],"df":"hard","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V122","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A company's policy index is rebuilt from scratch every Sunday. Policies change several times a week, and the assistant sometimes cites a policy that was withdrawn days earlier.\n\nWhich TWO changes improve freshness? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rebuild the index twice a week instead of once, keeping the full rebuild process unchanged"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask Claude to warn users that any policy it cites may be out of date"},{"l":"C","t":"Increase the number of retrieved passages so that the current version is likely to appear too"},{"l":"D","t":"Store each passage's effective date and version, and prefer the current version in results"},{"l":"E","t":"Index document changes incrementally as they are published, including deletions"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Grounding quality depends on index freshness. Event-driven, incremental indexing that also processes deletions, plus version and date metadata used in ranking, keeps withdrawn content out of answers. Periodic rebuilds and disclaimers leave the window open.","x":["Incorrect. Halving the gap still leaves days of stale answers.","Incorrect. A blanket warning does not stop withdrawn policies being cited.","Incorrect. Retrieving more still includes the withdrawn version.","Correct. Version metadata lets retrieval avoid superseded text.","Correct. Updates and removals reach the index within minutes."],"df":"medium","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V123","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"After a retrieval change, the team sees the share of answers with at least one citation rise from 70% to 95%. Users report no improvement, and spot checks show many citations point to passages that do not support the cited claim.\n\nWhat does this indicate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Citing is not grounding; check each citation supports its claim"},{"l":"B","t":"The change worked: answers are now grounded"},{"l":"C","t":"Users are not reading the citations, so the team should make them more visible in the interface"},{"l":"D","t":"The retrieval change should be rolled back, since more citations always means lower quality"}],"a":["A"],"r":"A metric that counts citations can rise while grounding gets worse. Groundedness asks whether each claim is supported by the passage it cites, which needs claim-level checks by humans or a calibrated judge. Measure the property, not its proxy.","x":["Correct. Counting citations rewards citing anything, not citing correctly.","Incorrect. Spot checks show many citations do not support their claims.","Incorrect. Visibility does not fix citations that support nothing.","Incorrect. Rollback without measuring support may discard a real gain."],"df":"hard","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V124","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An insurer's claims assistant answers questions from 2,000 long policy documents. Accuracy on questions that need numbers from tables is 58%, while prose questions reach 90%. Chunks are cut every 500 tokens regardless of structure.\n\nWhich TWO changes are most likely to help? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the chunk size to 5,000 tokens so that tables are never split, whatever their size"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove tables from the index so that prose answers, which score well, are not affected by them"},{"l":"C","t":"Store tables in a structured form that retrieval can match by row and column labels"},{"l":"D","t":"Have Claude estimate the numbers from the prose around each table when it cannot find them"},{"l":"E","t":"Chunk by document structure so that each table stays whole, with its headings attached"}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"When failures cluster in one content type, look at how that content is processed. Structure-aware chunking keeps tables intact with their headings, and structured representations allow exact matching on labels. Blindly larger chunks or estimation trade one failure for another.","x":["Incorrect. Huge chunks dilute relevance and cost more for every query.","Incorrect. Dropping tables makes those questions unanswerable.","Correct. Structured tables let exact lookups replace fuzzy matches on numbers.","Incorrect. Estimated figures in claims answers are a serious error.","Correct. Tables split across chunks lose the row and column labels the numbers need."],"df":"hard","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V126","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to know whether its retrieval stage or its answer generation is responsible for a drop in answer quality after a deployment.\n\nWhich TWO measurements separate the stages? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Recall at k: how often the passage that holds the answer is among the retrieved results"},{"l":"B","t":"The number of user sessions per day before and after the deployment"},{"l":"C","t":"Groundedness of answers in the retrieved passages"},{"l":"D","t":"Overall thumbs-up rate from users, compared week over week"},{"l":"E","t":"Average answer length before and after the deployment, compared across all questions"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Stage-level metrics locate a regression. Recall at k isolates retrieval, while groundedness of answers relative to retrieved passages isolates generation. End-to-end satisfaction and volume figures cannot tell the stages apart.","x":["Correct. This measures retrieval independently of what Claude writes.","Incorrect. Traffic volume says nothing about which stage regressed.","Correct. This measures generation given what retrieval provided.","Incorrect. A single satisfaction rate mixes both stages together.","Incorrect. Length changes do not show which stage failed."],"df":"medium","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V127","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A research assistant uses web search with allowed_domains set to four government and standards sites. Users ask about regulations from a fifth body whose site is not on the list, and the assistant answers from forum posts it found through a general search tool that the team added later.\n\nWhat is the most consistent fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the general tool but label forum posts as \"community sources\" in each answer"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the allowed_domains setting so that both search tools return the same broad results"},{"l":"C","t":"Apply the same source policy to every search path, adding the fifth body's site to the allowed list"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask Claude to prefer government sources in its answers whenever more than one source is available"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Source restrictions such as allowed_domains define what an answer may be grounded in. A second unrestricted search tool quietly bypasses that policy, so every retrieval path must enforce the same rules, with approved sources added explicitly.","x":["Incorrect. Labels do not make unofficial sources acceptable here.","Incorrect. Dropping the list gives up the source quality it exists to protect.","Correct. Grounding rules mean little if another tool bypasses them.","Incorrect. A preference does not stop forum posts being used."],"df":"hard","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V129","s":1,"d":2,"ty":"single","q":"A product-documentation assistant serves 15 product lines. Questions about one product often retrieve passages about a similarly named product, and users are told the wrong settings.\n\nWhat is the most effective first fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Retrieve twice as many passages so that the right product's passages are included too"},{"l":"B","t":"Merge the documentation for all 15 products into one long document per topic"},{"l":"C","t":"Add a sentence to the system prompt asking Claude to check which product each passage is about"},{"l":"D","t":"Tag passages by product and filter retrieval to the product the user is asking about"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Scope filters on metadata such as product, version or tenant are a cheap, precise way to stop near-duplicate content from other scopes being retrieved. Prompt instructions act too late, after the wrong passages are already in context.","x":["Incorrect. More passages raise cost and keep the confusing ones.","Incorrect. Merging makes it harder to tell products apart.","Incorrect. The wrong passages still crowd out the right ones.","Correct. A metadata filter removes passages from the wrong product before ranking."],"df":"medium","ob":"Retrieval and Knowledge Grounding at Scale"},
{"id":"V134","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An organization rolls out Claude Code to 900 engineers and wants usage, cost and tool-decision telemetry in its existing observability stack. Its security policy forbids collecting prompt text.\n\nWhich TWO configuration choices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask each engineer to export their session transcripts weekly and upload them to a shared drive"},{"l":"B","t":"Turn on prompt logging so that cost can be attributed to the kind of work in each prompt"},{"l":"C","t":"Use a screen-recording tool on engineers' machines to measure how Claude Code is being used"},{"l":"D","t":"Leave prompt logging off, since Claude Code does not include prompt text unless it is opted in"},{"l":"E","t":"Enable Claude Code's OpenTelemetry export to the organization's collector through managed settings"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Claude Code can export OpenTelemetry metrics and events, configured centrally through managed settings, and it logs prompt text only when that is explicitly enabled. That gives usage, cost and tool-decision visibility while respecting a no-prompt-content policy.","x":["Incorrect. Manual uploads are incomplete and carry the prompt content the policy forbids.","Incorrect. Prompt logging is exactly what the policy forbids.","Incorrect. Screen recording is invasive and captures far more than usage data.","Correct. Prompt content stays out of telemetry by default.","Correct. Central settings send the telemetry where the stack already runs."],"df":"hard","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V136","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A platform team is defining a standard event schema for every Claude call that services emit, so that logs from 20 services can be queried together.\n\nWhich TWO fields belong in the standard? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Model snapshot ID and prompt version"},{"l":"B","t":"The service's last editor"},{"l":"C","t":"Request and trace identifiers that join the event to its trace"},{"l":"D","t":"The raw API key used for the call, so that usage can be attributed to a key"},{"l":"E","t":"A free-text message written differently by each service team"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"A shared schema for structured log events should carry consistent, queryable fields: identifiers that join events to traces, and the versions (model snapshot, prompt) that explain behavior. Secrets must never be logged, and free text breaks cross-service queries.","x":["Correct. These explain behavior changes after model or prompt releases.","Incorrect. Code ownership belongs in the repository, not on every request event.","Correct. Identifiers link log events across services and spans.","Incorrect. Logging API keys exposes secrets.","Incorrect. Free text defeats the purpose of a queryable schema."],"df":"medium","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V137","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"Cache hit rate for a document-analysis service dropped from 80% to 5% after a release. No errors were reported, and the cached prefix is supposed to be the instructions plus a 900-token style guide.\n\nWhich finding best explains the drop?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The cache lifetime was reset to one hour, which caches fewer requests than five minutes would"},{"l":"B","t":"The release shortened the instructions, so the prefix fell below the model's minimum cacheable length"},{"l":"C","t":"Output tokens grew after the release, and longer outputs cannot be cached at all"},{"l":"D","t":"The service began streaming its responses, and streaming disables prompt caching for every request"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Prompt caching requires a minimum prefix length that depends on the model. A prefix that falls below it is silently not cached: requests succeed but cache_creation and cache_read counts stay at zero. Watching those usage fields is how a release like this is caught.","x":["Incorrect. A longer lifetime keeps entries longer; it does not stop caching.","Correct. Below the minimum, nothing is cached and no error is raised.","Incorrect. Caching applies to the input prefix, not output length.","Incorrect. Streaming and caching work together."],"df":"hard","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V138","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An agent's traces record each tool call's name and duration. During an incident, engineers could not tell which tool calls failed or why the agent stopped.\n\nWhich TWO attributes should each span carry? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The number of characters in each tool's description, recorded on every call"},{"l":"B","t":"The tool result's error flag and error category for tool spans"},{"l":"C","t":"A random sampling weight"},{"l":"D","t":"The complete tool input and output on every span, for every user, by default"},{"l":"E","t":"The stop reason on model-call spans"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Spans should carry the attributes that explain behavior: for tools, whether the result was an error and its category; for model calls, the stop reason, tokens and cache usage. Content capture by default is a separate, governed decision.","x":["Incorrect. Description length does not change between calls.","Correct. Engineers can filter failed tool calls and group them by cause.","Incorrect. Weights do not explain failures.","Incorrect. Full content by default adds cost and privacy exposure.","Correct. The stop reason shows why each turn ended."],"df":"hard","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V141","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A team's dashboards show high error rates for a Claude-backed endpoint but cannot separate rate-limit errors from overload errors or invalid requests.\n\nWhat should the instrumentation record?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The number of retries each call made, without the error that caused them"},{"l":"B","t":"The full error message text, searched by engineers when they investigate an alert"},{"l":"C","t":"The API error type or HTTP status for each failed call, as a structured field"},{"l":"D","t":"Only whether each call succeeded or failed, to keep the error metric simple"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Different errors call for different responses, so telemetry must keep them apart: a structured error type or status on each failed call lets dashboards and alerts distinguish rate limits, overloads and invalid requests.","x":["Incorrect. Retry counts do not say what failed.","Incorrect. Searching free text is slow and inconsistent.","Correct. Typed errors can be counted and alerted on separately.","Incorrect. A single failure count hides very different causes."],"df":"easy","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V143","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An agent framework emits spans, but tool calls made by subagents appear as separate traces that cannot be linked to the coordinator's request.\n\nWhat is the fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Propagate the coordinator's trace context into each subagent's spans"},{"l":"B","t":"Give each subagent its own sampling rate so that its traces are more likely to be kept"},{"l":"C","t":"Disable subagent tracing to avoid confusion, keeping only the coordinator's spans"},{"l":"D","t":"Merge all subagent logs into one file per day so that engineers can search them together"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Distributed tracing depends on propagating trace context across boundaries, including into subagents and tool services. Without it, each component produces orphaned traces that cannot be assembled into one request.","x":["Correct. Context propagation links subagent work to the parent request.","Incorrect. Sampling does not connect traces.","Incorrect. Dropping subagent spans hides where work happened.","Incorrect. A shared file does not link spans to the request."],"df":"medium","ob":"Telemetry and Instrumentation"},
{"id":"V146","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team is defining SLIs for a customer-facing Claude assistant. The current dashboard shows only HTTP error rate and average latency.\n\nWhich TWO SLIs add the most useful coverage? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Time to first token at the 95th percentile"},{"l":"B","t":"The number of tokens processed per day across the whole service"},{"l":"C","t":"Average CPU utilization of the service's application servers"},{"l":"D","t":"The count of deployments made to the service each week"},{"l":"E","t":"Share of sampled conversations passing a rubric-based quality check"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"LLM services fail in ways HTTP error rates miss: slow starts and wrong or poor answers that return 200. Tail time-to-first-token and a quality SLI from sampled evaluation cover those, while volume and infrastructure metrics do not describe the user's experience.","x":["Correct. Users judge responsiveness by when text starts appearing.","Incorrect. Volume is a capacity figure, not a measure of user experience.","Incorrect. Server CPU does not reflect what users experience.","Incorrect. Deployment counts are a delivery metric, not a service level indicator.","Correct. A quality SLI catches failures that return HTTP 200."],"df":"medium","ob":"Production Monitoring and Alerting"},
{"id":"V155","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A monitoring rule compares this week's mean answer length with last week's and alerts on a 20% change. It fires every Monday, because weekend traffic is dominated by short questions.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the threshold to 60% so that the Monday effect stays below the alert level"},{"l":"B","t":"Suppress all alerts on Mondays so that the weekend traffic pattern stops causing pages"},{"l":"C","t":"Compare like with like: same day last week, per segment"},{"l":"D","t":"Delete the rule entirely"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Drift detection must account for seasonality and traffic mix. Comparing the same period and segment removes false alarms caused by predictable patterns, while keeping the signal for real behavior changes.","x":["Incorrect. A huge threshold misses real changes.","Incorrect. Blanket suppression hides real Monday problems.","Correct. Seasonal and mix effects must be removed from the comparison.","Incorrect. Length shifts are a real drift signal after model changes."],"df":"hard","ob":"Production Monitoring and Alerting"},
{"id":"V156","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An organization wants a single dashboard for executives on its Claude deployments. Engineers already have detailed operational dashboards.\n\nWhich TWO views fit the executive dashboard? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Per-span tool latency"},{"l":"B","t":"A list of every prompt version deployed this month"},{"l":"C","t":"SLO attainment per product"},{"l":"D","t":"Cost per successful outcome by product"},{"l":"E","t":"Raw counts of each API error type per minute"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Different audiences need different dashboards. Executives need outcome and commitment views, such as cost per successful outcome and SLO attainment; engineers need the detailed spans, error types and releases.","x":["Incorrect. Span-level detail belongs on engineering dashboards.","Incorrect. Release detail is for engineering and change records.","Correct. It shows whether services meet their commitments.","Correct. It ties spend to value in terms executives use.","Incorrect. Minute-level error counts are operational, not executive."],"df":"medium","ob":"Production Monitoring and Alerting"},
{"id":"V158","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An agent platform produces 4 million traces a day and keeps a random 1% sample to control storage cost. When a rare failure affecting 0.05% of requests is reported, engineers find only a handful of matching traces, too few to see a pattern.\n\nWhich sampling strategy fits better?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the random sample to 2%"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep only the first trace of each hour, so that storage is predictable and evenly spread"},{"l":"C","t":"Stop sampling and keep every trace for a week before deleting all of them at once"},{"l":"D","t":"Tail-based sampling that keeps errors and slow traces"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Head-based random sampling decides before anyone knows whether a trace is interesting, so rare failures are mostly discarded. Tail-based sampling decides after the trace completes, keeping errors, slow traces and anomalies while sampling the routine ones.","x":["Incorrect. Doubling a random sample still keeps few rare failures.","Incorrect. Time-based sampling ignores which traces matter.","Incorrect. Keeping everything multiplies cost, and history is lost after a week.","Correct. Decisions made after the trace completes keep the rare failures."],"df":"hard","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V160","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to classify why 12,000 failed agent conversations failed last month. Reading them all would take weeks.\n\nWhat approach is most appropriate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Sort the failures by length and treat the longest 10% as representative of the rest"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask Claude to write one summary of all 12,000 conversations in a single request"},{"l":"C","t":"Have Claude label each failure by a fixed category list, checked on a human sample"},{"l":"D","t":"Read 50 failures by hand and assume the other 11,950 follow the same distribution of causes"}],"a":["C"],"r":"At scale, Claude can label traces against a defined taxonomy, but the labels need validation against a human-reviewed sample before they drive decisions. That combines coverage with trust in the categories.","x":["Incorrect. Length is not a valid basis for sampling causes.","Incorrect. One summary of everything loses the per-failure categories.","Correct. Model-assisted labeling scales if a human check confirms it.","Incorrect. A small sample can miss important categories."],"df":"medium","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V164","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"After reviewing traces, a team finds that 70% of an agent's failures in one week came from a single tool returning empty results for one tenant's data format. Individually each trace looked like an ordinary \"nothing found\" answer.\n\nWhat does this illustrate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Single traces are always sufficient, as long as engineers read enough of them closely"},{"l":"B","t":"Aggregating failures by tool, tenant and result type reveals issues invisible in single traces"},{"l":"C","t":"Empty tool results should never be shown to the agent, since they always indicate failures"},{"l":"D","t":"The tenant's data format is wrong and the tenant should be asked to change it"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Systemic issues often look normal one trace at a time. Aggregation by tool, tenant and result type turns many ordinary-looking traces into a visible pattern, which is the core of trace analysis at scale.","x":["Incorrect. Each trace here looked normal on its own.","Correct. The pattern only shows up when traces are grouped.","Incorrect. Empty results can be legitimate answers.","Incorrect. The integration should handle the format or report an error."],"df":"medium","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V166","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"A team asks why it should keep all traces with errors even when sampling successful traces at 1%.\n\nWhat is the best reason?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Error traces are small and cheap to keep"},{"l":"B","t":"The API requires that every error trace be stored for audit purposes"},{"l":"C","t":"Successful traces cannot be analyzed, so there is no reason to keep many of them"},{"l":"D","t":"Errors are rare and the most useful for diagnosis"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Sampling should keep the traces that carry the most diagnostic value. Errors are rare and essential for diagnosis, so they are kept in full while routine successes are sampled.","x":["Incorrect. Error traces are not reliably smaller.","Incorrect. The API sets no such requirement.","Incorrect. Successful traces are useful as a comparison group.","Correct. Errors are where engineers most need full detail."],"df":"easy","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V167","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to measure how often its research agent's final reports include claims not supported by any retrieved source, across 50,000 reports a month.\n\nWhich TWO approaches fit this scale? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Review only the reports that users complained about, since those contain the problem"},{"l":"B","t":"Score a random sample of reports with a judge checking each claim against the retrieved sources"},{"l":"C","t":"Validate the judge against human reviewers on a subset before trusting its monthly figures"},{"l":"D","t":"Count the number of citations per report and treat reports with many citations as grounded"},{"l":"E","t":"Ask the agent to report how many of its claims were unsupported at the end of each report"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Measuring a property such as groundedness at scale combines sampling with a judge that checks each claim against its sources, and that judge must be validated against human review before its numbers are used.","x":["Incorrect. Complaints miss most unsupported claims.","Correct. Sampling makes claim-level checking affordable.","Correct. A validated judge makes the estimate trustworthy.","Incorrect. Citation counts do not show support.","Incorrect. Self-reports are not reliable evidence."],"df":"medium","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V169","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team is choosing how long to keep traces. Engineers want months of history for trend analysis; security wants minimal stored content.\n\nWhich TWO policies balance these needs? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep aggregated metrics and metadata for a long period, and detailed spans for a short one"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep full-content spans for a year"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep only traces from internal staff accounts, deleting all customer traces at once"},{"l":"D","t":"Delete everything after 24 hours to eliminate stored data about users"},{"l":"E","t":"Keep captured content briefly, under its own policy"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Tiered retention keeps long-lived aggregates and metadata for trends, short-lived detailed spans for debugging, and any captured content for the shortest period, under its own policy.","x":["Correct. Trends come from aggregates; detail is needed only recently.","Incorrect. Long content retention conflicts with security.","Incorrect. Customer traffic is what needs monitoring.","Incorrect. Trend analysis becomes impossible.","Correct. Content retention is minimized and governed on its own."],"df":"medium","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V170","s":1,"d":3,"ty":"single","q":"An analyst presents a chart showing that conversations where the agent used the search tool had 20% lower satisfaction. The team proposes removing the search tool.\n\nWhat is the main problem with this conclusion?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The search tool should be removed only if satisfaction falls below 50%"},{"l":"B","t":"The chart should show averages per day instead of across all conversations"},{"l":"C","t":"Questions that need search may be harder, so search may not be the cause"},{"l":"D","t":"Satisfaction is never a useful metric for agents, so the chart can be ignored"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Observational trace data is confounded by task difficulty: hard questions both need search and get lower ratings. Before acting, compare like-for-like cohorts or run a controlled experiment.","x":["Incorrect. A threshold does not address whether the tool causes the effect.","Incorrect. Changing the time grain does not address confounding.","Correct. Correlation in observational traces can reflect which tasks use the tool.","Incorrect. Satisfaction is useful when interpreted carefully."],"df":"medium","ob":"Trace Analysis at Scale"},
{"id":"V171","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A customer reports that the support agent gave a wrong refund amount. The team replays the exact request recorded in the trace (same model snapshot, system prompt, messages and tool results) ten times, and every replay returns the correct amount.\n\nWhat does this most strongly suggest?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The tool returned the wrong amount, since tools are the most common source of incorrect agent answers"},{"l":"B","t":"The recorded request differs from what the model actually received, or a later step altered the answer"},{"l":"C","t":"The model snapshot has been updated since the incident, so the bug has already been fixed by Anthropic"},{"l":"D","t":"The model is non-deterministic, so the wrong answer was a random one-off that needs no further action"}],"a":["B"],"r":"When an exact replay of the recorded request reliably gives the right answer, the model behaved correctly on that input. The likely origin is a mismatch between what was logged and what was sent, or a step after the model (formatting, post-processing, rendering). Replay isolates the layer.","x":["Incorrect. The replay included the recorded tool result and still produced the right amount.","Correct. Consistent correct replays point away from the model and toward the harness or downstream code.","Incorrect. The replay used the same pinned snapshot recorded in the trace.","Incorrect. Ten correct replays make a random model error unlikely, and dismissing it skips the investigation."],"df":"hard","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V172","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A multi-turn insurance assistant forgets a customer's stated deductible in turn 14 and quotes the wrong figure. In turn 3 the customer gave the deductible. The trace shows the harness truncated the history to the last 10 turns starting at turn 12.\n\nWhich TWO conclusions are supported? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The model hallucinated the figure because it lacks knowledge of insurance deductibles"},{"l":"B","t":"A larger model would remember the deductible across the truncated history"},{"l":"C","t":"The customer should restate important facts every few turns to keep them in context"},{"l":"D","t":"The fix is a context policy that keeps key facts, such as a structured summary, when trimming"},{"l":"E","t":"The harness's history policy dropped the turn holding the fact"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"In multi-turn failures, look for where information was lost before blaming the model. Here the harness's truncation removed the turn containing the deductible, so the fix is a context policy that preserves key facts, such as a running summary or structured state.","x":["Incorrect. The figure was in the conversation; it was removed before the request.","Incorrect. No model can recall text that was not sent to it.","Incorrect. The product should not depend on users working around its harness.","Correct. Trimming must preserve what later turns depend on.","Correct. The model never saw the deductible when it answered."],"df":"hard","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V177","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A tool-using agent works in the team's tests but fails in production with API errors saying that a tool_result block does not match any tool_use in the previous message.\n\nWhere is the problem most likely to be?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In the harness, which mismatches tool_use IDs"},{"l":"B","t":"In the model, which is generating tool_use IDs that the API does not recognize"},{"l":"C","t":"In the rate limits, which reject tool results when too many arrive in one request"},{"l":"D","t":"In the tool, which is too slow"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Every tool_result must reference the id of a tool_use block in the preceding assistant message. Errors about mismatches point to the harness: for example, dropping or reordering messages, or mixing results from parallel calls, often only under production concurrency.","x":["Correct. Matching IDs is the harness's job, and the error names exactly that.","Incorrect. IDs come from the API's own response.","Incorrect. Rate limits return rate-limit errors.","Incorrect. Slow tools do not change ID matching."],"df":"medium","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V178","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A grounded Q&A assistant answers 15% of questions about a newly launched product incorrectly. The team needs to know whether to fix retrieval, the prompt, or the data.\n\nWhich TWO checks most quickly locate the origin? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rewrite the system prompt to emphasize accuracy and measure the change next week"},{"l":"B","t":"Check whether the new product's documents are in the index and current"},{"l":"C","t":"For failed questions, check whether the right passage reached the model"},{"l":"D","t":"Switch to a larger model for questions that mention the new product"},{"l":"E","t":"Raise the number of retrieved passages from 5 to 50 for every question"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Grounding failures have an origin in data, retrieval or generation. Checking whether the documents exist and are current, then whether the right passage reached the model, locates the stage before anything is changed.","x":["Incorrect. Changing the prompt before diagnosis may not address the cause.","Correct. Missing or stale data is a common origin for new-product failures.","Correct. This separates retrieval failures from generation failures.","Incorrect. A model cannot use documents that were never retrieved.","Incorrect. Retrieving more does not help if the documents are not indexed."],"df":"medium","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V180","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A coordinator agent's summaries began omitting findings from one specialist after a refactor. The specialist's traces show complete findings. The coordinator's request logs show its input contains only the first 2,000 characters of that specialist's output.\n\nWhich TWO conclusions are correct? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The fix belongs in the handoff code, for example passing the full structured result"},{"l":"B","t":"The coordinator needs a larger context window to read the specialist's findings"},{"l":"C","t":"The coordinator model is ignoring the specialist because its findings are less relevant"},{"l":"D","t":"The specialist should be told to write shorter findings so that they fit the limit"},{"l":"E","t":"The harness truncates the specialist's output before passing it to the coordinator"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Compare what each agent produced with what the next agent received. A complete output on one side and a truncated input on the other locates the failure in the handoff code, not in either model.","x":["Correct. The fault is in the integration between agents.","Incorrect. The truncation happened before the context window was involved.","Incorrect. The coordinator cannot use findings that were cut off.","Incorrect. Shortening hides the harness bug rather than fixing it.","Correct. The coordinator never received the full findings."],"df":"hard","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V183","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A support agent answers correctly in English but gives inconsistent policy details in Spanish. Retrieval returns the same English policy documents for both languages. Spanish answers translate figures and sometimes convert units wrongly.\n\nWhere should the team look first?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"At generation: translating figures from English sources"},{"l":"B","t":"At retrieval: Spanish queries must be retrieving different and less accurate documents"},{"l":"C","t":"At the data: outdated English documents"},{"l":"D","t":"At the model tier: Claude cannot answer questions in Spanish to a production standard"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Hold the evidence constant and compare outputs. Identical retrieval with different answer quality points to generation, here the translation and unit conversion step, which can be addressed with instructions, localized sources or structured figures.","x":["Correct. The evidence is the same, so the difference is in how answers are produced.","Incorrect. The trace shows the same documents are retrieved.","Incorrect. The same documents give correct English answers.","Incorrect. The problem is translation of figures and units, not Spanish itself."],"df":"hard","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V184","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An agent sometimes loops, calling the same search tool with the same query up to 40 times until its turn limit. The search tool returns a 200-word result each time.\n\nWhich TWO investigations are most useful? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Check whether the turn limit is too low, since a higher limit would let the agent finish"},{"l":"B","t":"Check whether the harness actually sends each tool result back, or drops some under load"},{"l":"C","t":"Check whether the users are asking too many questions in the same conversation"},{"l":"D","t":"Check whether the model is too small to understand search results of 200 words"},{"l":"E","t":"Check whether the tool result looks complete and successful to the agent"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Repeated identical tool calls usually mean the agent is not getting what it expects: the result is ambiguous or looks truncated, or the harness is not returning it at all. Both are checked in the trace before changing models or limits.","x":["Incorrect. A higher limit just lets the loop run longer.","Correct. If results never arrive, the agent keeps asking.","Incorrect. The loop happens within one task.","Incorrect. Results of that length are well within any model's ability.","Correct. An ambiguous result can make the agent think it must try again."],"df":"hard","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V185","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team classifies a set of production failures. In one case the model's answer was correct, but the web interface rendered a Markdown table incorrectly, so the user saw shifted columns.\n\nHow should this failure be classified?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"As a model output failure, since the model should avoid Markdown tables entirely"},{"l":"B","t":"As an application rendering failure, outside the model, harness, tools and data"},{"l":"C","t":"As a data failure, since the source data must have contained a shifted column"},{"l":"D","t":"As a retrieval failure, since the wrong document must have been retrieved"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Correct classification matters because it decides who fixes what. Here the model produced the right content and the presentation layer broke it, so the fix is in the interface, not in prompts or models.","x":["Incorrect. Tables were valid; the renderer mishandled them.","Correct. The model output was right; the display was wrong.","Incorrect. The underlying data was correct.","Incorrect. Retrieval returned the right content."],"df":"medium","ob":"Complex Failure Diagnosis"},
{"id":"V192","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team adds Claude as a first responder in its on-call channel. It reads alerts, dashboards and recent deploys, and can run the runbook's rollback step through a connected deploy tool.\n\nWhich TWO safeguards should be in place? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Claude's findings, actions and approvals are recorded in the incident thread"},{"l":"B","t":"Claude rolls back automatically whenever latency rises, to minimize time to mitigation"},{"l":"C","t":"Claude proposes a rollback and waits for the on-call engineer's approval before running it"},{"l":"D","t":"Claude's messages are posted in a private channel so that the on-call team is not distracted"},{"l":"E","t":"Claude is given administrator credentials for every production system to avoid permission errors"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"An AI first responder speeds triage, but consequential actions such as rollbacks should wait for human approval, and everything it finds and does should be visible in the incident record. Broad credentials and silent automation add risk.","x":["Correct. The thread becomes the audit record and timeline.","Incorrect. Unapproved automatic rollbacks can cause their own incidents.","Correct. A person stays accountable for production changes.","Incorrect. Hiding the investigation loses shared context.","Incorrect. Broad credentials widen the blast radius."],"df":"hard","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V194","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team's incident runbook for its agent platform covers infrastructure outages only. A recent incident was a quality regression with no errors: answers became subtly wrong after a retrieval index update.\n\nWhich TWO additions to the runbook would help most? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A rule that incidents are declared only when the error rate exceeds 5%"},{"l":"B","t":"Rollback steps for non-code changes, such as prompts, models and retrieval indexes"},{"l":"C","t":"A step requiring a full root-cause analysis before any mitigation is attempted"},{"l":"D","t":"Quality triggers, such as sampled-quality drops, and who declares them"},{"l":"E","t":"A list of every prompt used in the company, attached to the runbook"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"LLM systems have incident classes that infrastructure runbooks miss: quality regressions without errors. Runbooks need triggers for them and rollback procedures for prompts, models and indexes, which are often the cause.","x":["Incorrect. Quality regressions often have no error rate change.","Correct. These are the changes most likely to cause quality incidents.","Incorrect. Mitigation should not wait for root cause.","Correct. Quality incidents need defined triggers like outages do.","Incorrect. A prompt list does not tell responders what to do."],"df":"medium","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V198","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants each incident to improve its monitoring coverage. In the last incident, an agent's refusal rate tripled for six hours before anyone noticed.\n\nWhich TWO changes turn this into better coverage? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Wait for the next incident to see whether another signal is also missing"},{"l":"B","t":"Check similar signals for gaps, such as truncation and tool errors"},{"l":"C","t":"Add refusal rate per route to dashboards with an alert band and an owner"},{"l":"D","t":"Add a weekly meeting where engineers review refusal examples by hand"},{"l":"E","t":"Lower the refusal rate by instructing the model never to refuse"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Postmortems should ask what monitoring would have detected the problem sooner and add it, with an owner and alert. They should also look for analogous blind spots, because coverage gaps tend to come in families.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting repeats the cost of the gap.","Correct. One gap often points to others of the same kind.","Correct. The signal that would have caught it is now watched.","Incorrect. Manual reviews detect problems days late.","Incorrect. Removing refusals creates a safety problem."],"df":"medium","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V201","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team's incident timeline for a quality regression is assembled from memory a week later, and engineers disagree about when the change shipped and when symptoms began.\n\nWhat practice would prevent this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Record a timestamped timeline during the incident, from change events, alerts and actions"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the most senior engineer to write the timeline, since their memory is most reliable"},{"l":"C","t":"Skip the timeline and focus the postmortem only on action items"},{"l":"D","t":"Hold the postmortem meeting later, once everyone has had time to remember events"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Timelines should be reconstructed from evidence (deploy records, alerts, traces and incident-channel messages) and kept during the incident, not recalled afterwards.","x":["Correct. A live timeline built from evidence is accurate.","Incorrect. Seniority does not make memory accurate.","Incorrect. Without a timeline, causes and contributing factors are guesses.","Incorrect. Memories fade further with time."],"df":"medium","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V203","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"single","q":"A team's agent sends a misleading message to 3,000 customers before the issue is caught. The fix is deployed. Leadership asks what remains to be done.\n\nWhich follow-up is most important?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Nothing, since the fix is deployed and new customers will receive correct messages"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove the messaging feature permanently so that the issue cannot happen again"},{"l":"C","t":"Find the affected customers in the logs and send them a correction"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the affected customers to report whether they acted on the message"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Incident response includes remediation for those already affected: identifying them from logs and correcting the information, alongside the learning-loop actions that prevent recurrence.","x":["Incorrect. The 3,000 customers still hold the wrong information.","Incorrect. Removing a feature is disproportionate to a fixed bug.","Correct. Customers who received wrong information need correcting.","Incorrect. Shifting the burden to customers is inadequate."],"df":"hard","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V204","s":1,"d":4,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A quality regression in a medical-records summarizer was traced to a prompt change. The team rolled back within 40 minutes. In the postmortem, they find the change passed the evaluation suite, which contained no cases with medication dosages.\n\nWhich TWO contributing factors should the postmortem record? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The evaluation suite had a coverage gap for medication dosage cases"},{"l":"B","t":"The rollback took 40 minutes, which was too slow to matter at all"},{"l":"C","t":"The change was released to all traffic at once, without a staged rollout"},{"l":"D","t":"The engineer who wrote the prompt change did not have medical training"},{"l":"E","t":"The model is unsuitable for medical use and should be replaced with another provider"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Contributing factors describe system conditions: the evaluation suite missed a class of cases, and the release went to everyone at once. Both lead to concrete improvements, unlike blaming individuals or the model.","x":["Correct. The gate could not catch this failure class.","Incorrect. Forty minutes limited the harm, even if it can improve.","Correct. A canary would have limited the impact.","Incorrect. Personal blame misses the system factors.","Incorrect. The regression came from a prompt change."],"df":"hard","ob":"Incident Response and Learning Loops"},
{"id":"V211","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team's agent writes and runs code in a sandbox to answer data questions. They are designing behavioral checks for its evaluation suite.\n\nWhich TWO checks evaluate behavior rather than surface text? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Running the agent's final query against the database and comparing results with expected values"},{"l":"B","t":"Checking that the agent did not modify any table it was only supposed to read"},{"l":"C","t":"Comparing the agent's answer character by character with a reference answer written by an engineer"},{"l":"D","t":"Counting how many tool calls the agent made, and passing runs with fewer calls than average"},{"l":"E","t":"Checking that the agent's answer contains the word \"result\" somewhere in its final message"}],"a":["A","B"],"r":"Behavioral checks verify outcomes and side effects: whether the produced query returns the right data, and whether the agent stayed within its permissions. Text-matching and activity counts are weak proxies.","x":["Correct. The check verifies what the output does.","Correct. A behavioral check verifies what the agent did, not what it said.","Incorrect. Exact matching penalizes correct answers phrased differently.","Incorrect. Fewer calls does not mean a correct result.","Incorrect. A keyword check is a surface text test."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluation System Design"},
{"id":"V214","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants release criteria for changing an agent's model from one snapshot to a newer one.\n\nWhich criterion is most appropriate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"No category may fall below its quality floor, and cost and latency must stay within stated budgets"},{"l":"B","t":"The new snapshot must be cheaper per token than the current one, since cost is what migrations aim to improve"},{"l":"C","t":"The new snapshot must score higher overall than the current one on the suite, whatever happens per category"},{"l":"D","t":"The new snapshot must pass a review by the engineer who knows the current prompts best"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Release criteria for model changes should combine per-category quality floors with cost and latency budgets. Overall averages and token price alone can approve a change that harms a category or raises total cost.","x":["Correct. The criteria cover quality per category plus operational limits.","Incorrect. Cheaper tokens can still cost more if outputs grow.","Incorrect. An overall gain can hide a category regression.","Incorrect. A single reviewer's opinion is not a release criterion."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluation System Design"},
{"id":"V215","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team maintains golden datasets for three agents. Product requirements change every quarter, and some golden answers are now wrong.\n\nWhich TWO practices keep the golden sets valid? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give each golden set an owner who reviews it when requirements change"},{"l":"B","t":"Never change a golden answer once written, so that results stay comparable forever"},{"l":"C","t":"Delete cases whenever the current release fails them, since they may be out of date"},{"l":"D","t":"Regenerate all golden answers with the current model each quarter, so that they match its behavior"},{"l":"E","t":"Version the golden sets, and record which version each evaluation result was measured on"}],"a":["A","E"],"r":"Golden sets encode requirements, so they need owners, review when requirements change, and versioning so results can be compared. Labels generated by the model under test, or deleted whenever they fail, destroy their value.","x":["Correct. Ownership ties the set to the requirements it encodes.","Incorrect. Wrong labels make the comparisons meaningless.","Incorrect. Deleting failures removes the regressions the set should catch.","Incorrect. Model-generated labels grade the model against itself.","Correct. Results stay comparable and traceable over time."],"df":"medium","ob":"Evaluation System Design"},
{"id":"V217","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team's suite grades agent runs with checks that read files the agent left behind. A run was found to pass by editing the check script itself.\n\nWhich TWO changes protect the suite's validity? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the agent in its prompt not to modify any evaluation files during the run"},{"l":"B","t":"Run checks from a copy made before the agent runs, outside the paths it may change"},{"l":"C","t":"Run the checks before the agent starts, so that its edits cannot affect them"},{"l":"D","t":"Fail any run that changes files outside the paths the case allows"},{"l":"E","t":"Hide the check files by giving them names the agent is unlikely to look for"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"An agent under evaluation must not be able to change what grades it. Keep checks outside the agent's reach, for example copied before the run, and fail runs that touch files outside their allowed scope.","x":["Incorrect. A prompt request does not prevent the edit.","Correct. The agent cannot alter the checks that grade it.","Incorrect. Checks must grade the agent's results, which exist only after it runs.","Correct. Out-of-scope edits, including to checks, fail the run.","Incorrect. Obscurity is not a control."],"df":"hard","ob":"Evaluation System Design"},
{"id":"V218","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team is starting an evaluation suite for a new extraction agent.\n\nWhat is the best first source of cases?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Public benchmark questions unrelated to the extraction task"},{"l":"B","t":"A handful of easy inputs that the engineer is confident the agent will pass"},{"l":"C","t":"Examples generated by the same model the agent uses, with outputs it produced"},{"l":"D","t":"Real inputs from the target workload, each with an accepted expected output"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Good suites start from real inputs with accepted outputs, typically 20 to 50 representative cases, then grow with edge cases and production failures.","x":["Incorrect. Unrelated benchmarks do not test this task.","Incorrect. Easy cases do not discriminate.","Incorrect. Self-generated cases grade the model against itself.","Correct. Real cases reflect what the agent must handle."],"df":"easy","ob":"Evaluation System Design"},
{"id":"V221","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team designs a rubric for an LLM judge that scores support replies. In a pilot, two human raters using the same rubric disagree on 40% of replies.\n\nWhich TWO changes should come first? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use only the more senior rater's labels, since senior raters are more accurate than junior ones"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask the judge to score on a 1 to 100 scale, so that finer gradations capture the disagreement"},{"l":"C","t":"Measure agreement between the human raters again before calibrating the judge against them"},{"l":"D","t":"Deploy the judge now and average its scores with both raters' scores for a final grade"},{"l":"E","t":"Rewrite vague criteria into specific, observable checks with examples of each score"}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"A judge can only be as consistent as the rubric allows. If humans disagree, tighten the rubric into specific, observable criteria with anchored examples and re-measure inter-rater agreement before calibrating any judge.","x":["Incorrect. Seniority does not make vague criteria clear.","Incorrect. A finer scale with vague criteria adds noise.","Correct. The judge cannot be calibrated to labels that disagree.","Incorrect. Averaging over inconsistent labels hides the problem.","Correct. Clear criteria raise agreement between any raters, human or model."],"df":"medium","ob":"LLM-as-Judge Engineering"},
{"id":"V222","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"An LLM judge scores longer answers higher than shorter answers of equal quality, according to a human-labeled comparison set.\n\nWhat is the best mitigation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask the generator to write longer answers, since the judge will then score them more highly"},{"l":"B","t":"Truncate all answers to the same length before the judge sees them, so it cannot prefer the longer one"},{"l":"C","t":"Add rubric criteria that give no credit for length, and check the bias again on the labeled set"},{"l":"D","t":"Use a larger model as the judge, since larger models never show a preference for length"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Verbosity bias is a known judge failure. Rubrics that explicitly give no credit for length, followed by measurement against human labels, address it. Truncation and larger judges are not reliable fixes.","x":["Incorrect. That optimizes for the bias instead of quality.","Incorrect. Truncation removes content the judge needs to assess.","Correct. The rubric removes the incentive, and measurement confirms it.","Incorrect. Larger judges can show the same bias."],"df":"medium","ob":"LLM-as-Judge Engineering"},
{"id":"V223","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team uses Claude as both the generator and the judge for a writing assistant. Comparing against human ratings, the judge scores Claude's outputs higher than a competitor model's outputs that humans prefer.\n\nWhich TWO steps address this? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Tell the judge not to favor any model"},{"l":"B","t":"Use a judge from a different model family"},{"l":"C","t":"Keep the judge, since self-preference does not affect comparisons between prompt versions"},{"l":"D","t":"Measure judge scores against human labels separately for each source model"},{"l":"E","t":"Replace human labels with judge scores, since the judge is more consistent than people are"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Self-preference bias appears when a judge rates outputs from its own model family higher. Measure agreement per source and, where comparisons include the generator's model, use a judge from a different model.","x":["Incorrect. The judge usually cannot tell which model wrote an output.","Correct. A different judge reduces self-preference.","Incorrect. Self-preference distorts any comparison across models.","Correct. Per-source agreement reveals whether the judge favors its own outputs.","Incorrect. Consistency without accuracy is not calibration."],"df":"hard","ob":"LLM-as-Judge Engineering"},
{"id":"V230","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team's LLM judge agrees with human labels 92% of the time overall. On cases involving refund policy exceptions, agreement is 55%.\n\nWhat should the team conclude?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The judge is reliable, since 92% overall agreement is well above the usual threshold"},{"l":"B","t":"Refund exception cases are too rare to matter, so they can be removed from the suite"},{"l":"C","t":"The human labels for refund exceptions are probably wrong, since the judge is right 92% of the time"},{"l":"D","t":"The judge is not reliable for refund exceptions; fix its rubric there or keep humans on those cases"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Judges can be well calibrated overall and unreliable in specific categories. Gating authority should follow category-level agreement, with rubric work or human review where agreement is low.","x":["Incorrect. The overall figure hides a category it fails.","Incorrect. Rare but consequential cases are often what matter most.","Incorrect. The judge is the instrument being calibrated against humans.","Correct. Category-level agreement shows where the judge can be trusted."],"df":"hard","ob":"LLM-as-Judge Engineering"},
{"id":"V233","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team runs an A/B test of a new prompt for a travel-booking assistant. After one day, variant B shows a 4% higher booking rate, and the product manager wants to ship it. Weekend traffic behaves differently from weekday traffic, and the planned test length was two weeks.\n\nWhat should the team do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ship variant B to half of traffic permanently, so that both variants keep running side by side"},{"l":"B","t":"Ship variant B now, since a 4% higher booking rate is a large improvement for any travel product"},{"l":"C","t":"Stop the test and run a new one with only weekend traffic, to remove the weekday effect"},{"l":"D","t":"Run the test for its planned length, then check whether the difference is significant"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A/B tests need a planned duration that covers traffic cycles and a significance check at the end. Stopping when a variant looks good (peeking) inflates false positives, especially with known day-of-week effects.","x":["Incorrect. Running both indefinitely avoids the decision.","Incorrect. One day's result may be noise or a weekday effect.","Incorrect. Restarting does not answer the original question.","Correct. Stopping early on a good day inflates false positives."],"df":"hard","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V234","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to measure the quality of a document-extraction service on live traffic.\n\nWhich TWO metrics fit this task type? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"User thumbs-up rate shown after each extraction in the review interface"},{"l":"B","t":"Field-level accuracy on sampled live documents against verified values"},{"l":"C","t":"The rate of downstream corrections made by staff to extracted fields"},{"l":"D","t":"The average number of words in each extracted field across all documents"},{"l":"E","t":"A judge's 1 to 10 rating of how fluent the extracted fields read to a human"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Online metrics must match the task type. Extraction is measured by field-level accuracy against verified values and by downstream correction rates, not by fluency or length.","x":["Incorrect. Sparse ratings are weak for structured accuracy.","Correct. Extraction quality is measured field by field against truth.","Correct. Corrections are a direct production signal of errors.","Incorrect. Word counts do not measure correctness.","Incorrect. Fluency is irrelevant for structured fields."],"df":"medium","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V235","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"An agent's online quality is measured by thumbs-up and thumbs-down buttons. Only 2% of users click either, and most clicks are thumbs-down from frustrated users.\n\nHow should the team use this signal?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Discard it entirely, since feedback from a small share of users carries no information"},{"l":"B","t":"Show the buttons more often until at least half of users click, then use the rate as is"},{"l":"C","t":"As one input with known bias, alongside sampled quality evaluation of representative traffic"},{"l":"D","t":"As the main quality metric, since direct user feedback is always the most accurate signal"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Explicit feedback is valuable but sparse and self-selected. It flags problems and examples, while a sampled, rubric-based evaluation of representative traffic provides the unbiased quality estimate.","x":["Incorrect. Feedback still flags real problems.","Incorrect. Prompting users more changes who responds, not the bias.","Correct. Sparse, skewed feedback needs a representative complement.","Incorrect. A 2% self-selected sample is not representative."],"df":"medium","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V236","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team runs an A/B test comparing two agent configurations. Variant B has higher task completion but also 12% more escalations to human agents.\n\nWhich TWO practices help the team decide? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Extend the test indefinitely until one variant wins on every metric"},{"l":"B","t":"Define a primary metric and guardrail metrics before the test, with limits for each"},{"l":"C","t":"Pick whichever variant the team prefers, since both have one better metric"},{"l":"D","t":"Measure cost per successful outcome, including the cost of escalations"},{"l":"E","t":"Ship B, since task completion is what users care about and escalations are an internal cost"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Online experiments need a pre-declared primary metric and guardrails, so trade-offs are judged by rules set in advance. Combining outcomes into cost per success makes trade-offs between metrics explicit.","x":["Incorrect. Variants rarely dominate on all metrics.","Correct. Pre-registered guardrails prevent cherry-picking after the fact.","Incorrect. Preference is not a decision rule.","Correct. One figure weighs completion against escalation cost.","Incorrect. Escalations affect users and cost."],"df":"hard","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V239","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team ships a prompt change that offline evaluation rated slightly better. Live metrics a week later show no difference in resolution rate, but users now take longer to get answers because responses are longer.\n\nWhat does this suggest about the team's measurement?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The team should stop running online measurement, since offline evaluation already showed improvement"},{"l":"B","t":"The offline evaluation must have been run incorrectly, since offline and online results always agree"},{"l":"C","t":"Offline gains need confirming online, with latency and effort measured too"},{"l":"D","t":"Resolution rate is the only metric that matters, so the change is neutral and can stay"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Offline evaluation reduces risk; online measurement confirms value. Online metrics should cover user effort and latency as well as task outcomes, because changes can be neutral on one and harmful on another.","x":["Incorrect. Online measurement is what caught the cost.","Incorrect. Offline and online results often differ.","Correct. Live metrics show costs that offline scores missed.","Incorrect. Slower answers are a real cost to users."],"df":"hard","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V241","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A product team uses a judge to score a random sample of live conversations each day for helpfulness. The daily score dropped 6% on the day a large enterprise customer onboarded 4,000 new users.\n\nWhat should the team check first?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Whether the traffic mix changed, by comparing scores within segments before and after the day"},{"l":"B","t":"Whether to roll back the most recent prompt change, to restore the earlier score"},{"l":"C","t":"Whether the judge is broken, since a 6% drop in one day is too large to be real"},{"l":"D","t":"Whether the model snapshot changed that day, since model changes are the most common cause of drops"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Online quality metrics move with traffic mix. When a large new cohort arrives, compare scores within segments to see whether quality changed or the population did.","x":["Correct. A new cohort can move the average without any regression.","Incorrect. Rolling back without diagnosis may not address the cause.","Incorrect. Large shifts can be real when traffic changes.","Incorrect. Check the mix change that coincides with the drop first."],"df":"medium","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V243","s":1,"d":5,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants an online metric for a retrieval-grounded Q&A assistant.\n\nWhich metric fits best?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The share of answers that include at least one bullet point"},{"l":"B","t":"The number of documents in the knowledge base this month"},{"l":"C","t":"The average response length of answers over the last week"},{"l":"D","t":"Share of sampled answers whose claims the sources support"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Metric selection matches task type. For retrieval-grounded answers, the core online metric is groundedness on sampled live traffic.","x":["Incorrect. Formatting is not correctness.","Incorrect. Corpus size is not answer quality.","Incorrect. Length does not measure grounding.","Correct. Groundedness is the core quality property for this task."],"df":"easy","ob":"Online Quality Measurement"},
{"id":"V246","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A support platform sends every request to an Opus-class model. An evaluation shows a Haiku-class model meets the quality floor on 70% of requests (simple account questions), and a confidence check can identify those requests with 95% precision. The rest need the larger model.\n\nWhich design best reduces cost while keeping quality?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Route requests the check marks as simple to the smaller model and send the rest, plus failed checks, to the larger one"},{"l":"B","t":"Keep all traffic on the larger model but lower its effort level for every request to save output tokens"},{"l":"C","t":"Send every request to the smaller model first and retry on the larger one whenever a customer complains about the answer"},{"l":"D","t":"Move all traffic to the smaller model, since 70% of requests meet the floor and that is a majority of the traffic"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Model routing sends each request to the cheapest model that meets its quality floor, using a check validated on evaluation data. Cascades escalate on failed checks, not on complaints. Uniform choices either overspend or underserve the hard cases.","x":["Correct. Routing by evaluated difficulty keeps quality while moving most traffic to the cheaper tier.","Incorrect. Lower effort on the hard 30% risks quality, and simple ones still pay large-model prices.","Incorrect. Waiting for complaints lets poor answers reach customers first.","Incorrect. 30% of requests would fall below the floor."],"df":"hard","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V251","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A research tool answers 2,000 analyst questions a day. Each answer averages 300 input tokens of question, 15,000 tokens of retrieved context, and 2,500 output tokens. The team wants to cut cost by 40% without lowering quality.\n\nWhich TWO changes target the largest cost drivers? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Right-size outputs, since output tokens cost more"},{"l":"B","t":"Move every question to the largest model, since better answers mean fewer follow-up questions"},{"l":"C","t":"Store each answer in a database and never answer the same analyst twice in a day"},{"l":"D","t":"Retrieve fewer, better-ranked passages, guided by recall measurements"},{"l":"E","t":"Shorten the analysts' questions"}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Cost work starts with the token breakdown. Retrieved context dominates input, and output tokens cost more per token, so tighter retrieval and right-sized outputs give the largest savings.","x":["Correct. Output tokens are priced higher per token.","Incorrect. The largest model raises cost per token.","Incorrect. Analysts ask different questions, so this saves little.","Correct. Retrieved context is most of the input tokens.","Incorrect. Questions are a tiny share of tokens."],"df":"medium","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V253","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A cache diagnostics review for a customer-service agent shows cache creation tokens are high on nearly every request, and cache reads are low, although the system prompt and tools rarely change.\n\nWhich TWO causes should the team check? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Responses being streamed, which disables cache reads on most requests"},{"l":"B","t":"Tool definitions sent in a different order on different requests"},{"l":"C","t":"A timestamp or user ID placed before the cache breakpoint"},{"l":"D","t":"Too many users, since each user has a separate cache that never fills up"},{"l":"E","t":"Output length varying between requests, which makes each response cache separately"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"High creation with low reads means the prefix is changing. Common causes are volatile values before the breakpoint and non-deterministic ordering of tools or system blocks. Fix the prefix to make it byte-for-byte stable.","x":["Incorrect. Streaming does not disable caching.","Correct. Any difference in the prefix, including order, misses the cache.","Correct. A changing prefix creates a new cache entry every time.","Incorrect. The cache is keyed on the prefix, not on users.","Incorrect. Output does not affect input caching."],"df":"hard","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V255","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team wants to reduce Claude Code spend across 600 engineers without slowing them down. Usage analytics show most sessions use the most capable model at high effort, including for routine tasks.\n\nWhich TWO changes fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Make a mid-tier model the default"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask engineers by email to use cheaper models when they remember to"},{"l":"C","t":"One small weekly cap for all"},{"l":"D","t":"Set a moderate default effort level, keeping higher effort available for hard tasks"},{"l":"E","t":"Block the most capable model for everyone, so that no session can ever use it"}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Most usage runs on defaults. Sensible default models and effort levels move most spend, while still allowing escalation for hard tasks. Bans and flat caps block valuable work.","x":["Correct. Most usage runs on the default, so the default sets most of the cost.","Incorrect. Reminders have little effect on defaults.","Incorrect. A flat cap blocks real work for heavy, productive users.","Correct. Effort beyond what a task needs adds tokens and time.","Incorrect. Blocking it slows the hard tasks that need it."],"df":"medium","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V256","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"An agent that answers HR questions uses prompt caching on a 6,000-token system prompt. Traffic is 50 requests per minute. A new feature inserts each employee's name and department at the start of the system prompt.\n\nWhat happens to cost, and what is the fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The API rejects the requests, since personal data cannot be included in cached prompts"},{"l":"B","t":"Cost stays the same, since caching works per employee and each employee's prompt is reused"},{"l":"C","t":"Cost falls, since personalized prompts are cached more efficiently than generic ones"},{"l":"D","t":"Every request writes a new cache entry; move the personal details after the cached instructions"}],"a":["D"],"r":"A cache entry is shared only by requests with the same prefix. Putting per-user data at the start of the system prompt makes every prefix unique, so every request pays a cache write. Place stable content first and per-user content after the breakpoint.","x":["Incorrect. The requests are accepted; the cache is just not reused.","Incorrect. Each employee's prefix is reused far less than the shared one was.","Incorrect. Personalized prefixes reduce reuse.","Correct. A per-user prefix cannot be shared, so it must come after the cached part."],"df":"hard","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V257","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team's monthly Claude bill rose 60% while request volume rose 10%. A usage breakdown is available by model, by token type and by route.\n\nWhich TWO analyses most quickly explain the rise? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Change in the number of API keys in the organization between the two months"},{"l":"B","t":"Change in latency per request"},{"l":"C","t":"Change in the number of support tickets about the product between the months"},{"l":"D","t":"Change in model mix by route"},{"l":"E","t":"Change in tokens per request by token type and route, comparing the two months"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"When spend grows faster than volume, the cause is cost per request: more tokens per request or a pricier model mix. Breaking both down by route identifies which change drove the bill.","x":["Incorrect. Key counts do not change cost.","Incorrect. Latency is not a cost driver in itself.","Incorrect. Ticket counts do not explain token spend.","Correct. A move to a larger model raises cost without more requests.","Correct. A shift in tokens per request explains growth beyond volume."],"df":"hard","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V259","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An agent makes 40 independent lookups per task, each returning a large record, and then summarizes them. Programmatic tool calling is available on the team's platform for these tools.\n\nWhich TWO effects should the team expect from using it? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Fewer model round trips, since the script can make many tool calls in one step"},{"l":"B","t":"Fewer input tokens, since the records stay with the script and only its output reaches Claude"},{"l":"C","t":"Higher quality on tasks where Claude must reason about each result before choosing the next call"},{"l":"D","t":"Support for every tool setting, including strict tools and single-tool-per-turn settings"},{"l":"E","t":"Lower cost from code execution, which is free and not billed in any way"}],"a":["A","B"],"r":"Programmatic tool calling suits many independent lookups: a script runs the calls and only its printed result reaches Claude, cutting tokens and round trips. It helps less when each step depends on reasoning over the last result, and it has its own billing and limits.","x":["Correct. One script replaces many request-response cycles.","Correct. Large intermediate results no longer enter the context.","Incorrect. Dependent reasoning suits ordinary tool calls better.","Incorrect. Some tool settings are not supported with it.","Incorrect. Code execution time is billed separately."],"df":"medium","ob":"Cost Architecture at Scale"},
{"id":"V263","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A document Q&A service sends a 120,000-token contract with each question. Time to first token is 6 seconds at p95, and the target is 2 seconds. Users ask several questions about the same contract within a few minutes.\n\nWhich change best addresses time to first token?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Stream the response, since streaming reduces the time before the first token is produced"},{"l":"B","t":"Raise the effort level, so that Claude starts answering with more confidence"},{"l":"C","t":"Cache the contract prefix so that later questions reuse it instead of processing it again"},{"l":"D","t":"Lower max_tokens, since shorter answers start generating sooner than longer ones"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Time to first token is dominated by processing a long input. Caching the long, reused prefix lets later requests skip most of that work, which cuts time to first token for follow-up questions.","x":["Incorrect. Streaming shows tokens sooner once generated; it does not shorten prefill.","Incorrect. Higher effort usually adds latency.","Correct. Cached prefixes are processed much faster, which cuts time to first token.","Incorrect. Output limits do not affect the time to process the input."],"df":"hard","ob":"Latency Engineering"},
{"id":"V264","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A coding assistant runs at the highest effort level for all requests. Simple requests, such as renaming a variable, take 25 seconds, mostly in thinking before any output.\n\nWhich TWO changes improve latency on simple requests without hurting hard ones? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use the smallest model for everything"},{"l":"B","t":"Increase max_tokens, so that thinking completes sooner on every request"},{"l":"C","t":"Disable thinking entirely for all requests, since it is the main source of latency"},{"l":"D","t":"Keep higher effort for request types where evaluation shows it improves results"},{"l":"E","t":"Lower effort where evaluation shows it is not needed"}],"a":["D","E"],"r":"Effort level tuning trades reasoning depth for speed. Lower effort on request types that do not need it, keep higher effort where evaluation shows it pays, rather than one setting for everything.","x":["Incorrect. The smallest model may fail the hard requests.","Incorrect. A higher limit does not shorten thinking.","Incorrect. Disabling it everywhere weakens hard tasks.","Correct. Hard tasks keep the reasoning that helps them.","Correct. Less effort means less thinking time on simple work."],"df":"hard","ob":"Latency Engineering"},
{"id":"V269","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"A team's dashboard shows only total request latency for a chatbot. Users complain the bot \"takes forever to start\", but total latency is within target.\n\nWhat should the team add?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Requests per second, to see whether traffic volume explains the complaints"},{"l":"B","t":"Token counts per response, since longer answers start later than shorter ones"},{"l":"C","t":"Time to first token, so the start of the response is measured separately"},{"l":"D","t":"CPU usage on the application servers, to check for overloaded hardware"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Time to first token and total latency are different user experiences. A slow start with acceptable total time shows up only if time to first token is measured.","x":["Incorrect. Volume is not what users describe.","Incorrect. Output length affects total time, not the start.","Correct. Users are describing a slow start, which total latency does not show.","Incorrect. CPU usage does not measure what users see."],"df":"medium","ob":"Latency Engineering"},
{"id":"V273","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"single","q":"An agent's tool results are large JSON documents, and each turn's response time rises as they accumulate in context.\n\nWhich change most directly reduces per-turn latency?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Raise the effort level so that Claude processes the JSON more efficiently"},{"l":"B","t":"Stream tool results to Claude so that they are processed in parallel"},{"l":"C","t":"Increase the context window so that more tool results fit in each request"},{"l":"D","t":"Return only the fields the agent needs from each tool and clear old results"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Input size affects processing time for every turn. Compact tool results and clearing stale ones keep per-turn latency from growing as the task runs.","x":["Incorrect. Effort affects reasoning, not input size.","Incorrect. Tool results are sent as input, not streamed.","Incorrect. A larger window does not speed up processing.","Correct. Less input per turn means faster processing."],"df":"medium","ob":"Latency Engineering"},
{"id":"V274","s":1,"d":6,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team sets a latency budget for an agent: 3 seconds at p95 for the first visible response, 12 seconds for task completion.\n\nWhich TWO design choices support both targets? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run independent tool calls in parallel to shorten the path to task completion"},{"l":"B","t":"Use the largest model at top effort"},{"l":"C","t":"Put every tool call in a separate model turn, so that each turn finishes quickly"},{"l":"D","t":"Stream a short acknowledgment first, then finish the work"},{"l":"E","t":"Wait to show anything until every tool call has returned, so that output is complete"}],"a":["A","D"],"r":"Separate targets for first response and completion call for different techniques: stream early output for the first, and shorten the critical path, for example with parallel tool calls, for the second.","x":["Correct. Parallelism cuts completion time.","Incorrect. Highest effort everywhere slows both targets.","Incorrect. More turns lengthen completion time.","Correct. Users see progress within the first target.","Incorrect. Waiting risks missing the first-response target."],"df":"medium","ob":"Latency Engineering"},
{"id":"V276","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"An operations agent can run shell commands on build servers. A post-incident review found it ran a cleanup command that deleted a shared cache directory, which took four hours to rebuild. The system prompt already says \"never delete shared directories\".\n\nWhich control most reliably prevents a repeat?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A stronger system prompt that lists every shared directory by name and repeats the warning at the end of each turn"},{"l":"B","t":"A PostToolUse hook that logs every deleted directory so that the team can restore it from backup afterwards"},{"l":"C","t":"A PreToolUse hook that blocks destructive commands on protected paths before they run, with a reason shown to the agent"},{"l":"D","t":"A lower effort level, so that the agent takes fewer actions and is less likely to run cleanup commands at all"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Irreversible actions need deterministic enforcement outside the model. A PreToolUse hook (or permission deny rule) blocks the command before it runs and explains why. Prompts are advisory, logging is detective, and effort does not change capability.","x":["Incorrect. The prompt already said this, and a longer one is still advisory.","Incorrect. Logging after deletion detects the damage; it does not prevent it.","Correct. A deterministic check before execution holds whatever the model decides.","Incorrect. Less effort does not remove the capability to delete."],"df":"hard","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V277","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A customer-support agent handles chat and can look up orders. The team wants to stop customers' card numbers and social security numbers from being stored in logs or sent to downstream analytics.\n\nWhich TWO guardrails fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"A weekly script that searches the analytics store and deletes any card numbers it finds"},{"l":"B","t":"A redactor that masks those identifiers in messages before they are logged or forwarded"},{"l":"C","t":"A system prompt asking customers not to type card numbers into the chat window"},{"l":"D","t":"Turning off logging for the support agent so that no messages are ever stored anywhere"},{"l":"E","t":"An output check that masks them in replies before display or storage"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"PII protection works at the boundaries: redact inputs before they are logged or forwarded, and filter outputs before they are displayed or stored. Requests to users and after-the-fact cleanup leave exposure windows.","x":["Incorrect. The data has already spread by the time the script runs.","Correct. Masking at the boundary keeps the data out of downstream systems.","Incorrect. Customers will still type them.","Incorrect. Losing all logs blocks debugging and audit.","Correct. Replies are also a path for the data to leak."],"df":"medium","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V279","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A bank's agent can initiate wire transfers. Policy says transfers over $10,000 or to new payees require a human approver, and no transfer may exceed $250,000.\n\nWhich TWO controls implement this correctly? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"An approval gate in the tool path that pauses transfers over $10,000 or to new payees until approved"},{"l":"B","t":"A nightly report of large transfers"},{"l":"C","t":"A hard limit in the transfer tool rejecting anything over $250,000"},{"l":"D","t":"A rule that the agent may approve its own transfers when it is confident they are legitimate"},{"l":"E","t":"A system prompt stating both limits, with examples of transfers the agent should refuse"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Irreversible financial actions need deterministic enforcement: a hard cap in code, and an approval gate in the tool path for the cases that need a human. Prompts and next-day reports are not enforcement.","x":["Correct. The gate enforces approval where the action happens.","Incorrect. Review the next day does not stop an improper transfer.","Correct. A hard limit in code holds for every request.","Incorrect. Self-approval defeats the control.","Incorrect. Prompt rules can be bypassed by confused or injected turns."],"df":"hard","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V281","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team designs layered guardrails for an agent that reads customer emails and drafts replies, which staff then send.\n\nWhich TWO layers fit this risk profile? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Identity checks before reading email"},{"l":"B","t":"A rule that the agent may send replies directly when its confidence is high"},{"l":"C","t":"Screening incoming emails for prompt-injection patterns"},{"l":"D","t":"Output checks on drafts for leaked internal data or policy violations before staff see them"},{"l":"E","t":"An approval gate requiring a manager to approve every draft before staff may read it"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Layered guardrails match the risk profile: untrusted inputs are screened, outputs are checked before a human acts on them, and the human review step already in the design stays in place. Extra gates or self-sending shift the balance badly.","x":["Incorrect. Identity checks do not address content risks in emails.","Incorrect. Direct sending removes the human review the design relies on.","Correct. Untrusted email content is the main attack surface.","Correct. Drafts are checked before a person acts on them.","Incorrect. Staff already review drafts, so another gate adds delay."],"df":"medium","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V284","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to stop its coding agents from ever force-pushing to the main branch, across every developer machine and CI job.\n\nWhere should this rule live?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In personal settings files"},{"l":"B","t":"In managed settings, as a deny rule or hook"},{"l":"C","t":"In each repository's CLAUDE.md, as an instruction never to force-push to main"},{"l":"D","t":"In a wiki page that explains the risks of force-pushing to main"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Rules that must hold everywhere belong in managed settings, deployed centrally and not overridable, as permission deny rules or hooks. CLAUDE.md and wikis inform behavior but do not enforce it.","x":["Incorrect. Personal settings can be edited or forgotten.","Correct. Managed settings apply everywhere and cannot be switched off locally.","Incorrect. CLAUDE.md is guidance, not enforcement.","Incorrect. Documentation does not stop the command."],"df":"medium","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V285","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A healthcare agent's output filter for patient identifiers uses a strict pattern that also masks medication doses (numbers next to \"mg\"), which makes summaries useless to clinicians.\n\nWhich TWO steps should the team take? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the strict filter as it is"},{"l":"B","t":"Measure the filter's false-positive rate on labeled clinical samples, by entity type"},{"l":"C","t":"Refine detection, then confirm identifiers are still caught"},{"l":"D","t":"Disable the filter for clinicians, since clinicians are allowed to see patient data anyway"},{"l":"E","t":"Replace the filter with a prompt asking Claude not to include patient identifiers"}],"a":["B","C"],"r":"Guardrail tuning balances false positives and false negatives for the risk at hand. Measure both on labeled samples per entity type, refine detection, and confirm the true positives are still caught.","x":["Incorrect. Useless summaries create their own clinical risk.","Correct. The cost of over-masking must be quantified per entity.","Correct. The fix must keep catching true identifiers.","Incorrect. The filter also protects logs and downstream systems.","Incorrect. A prompt is not a dependable redaction control."],"df":"hard","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V288","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A team asks why guardrails for a production agent should be layered rather than relying on one strong check.\n\nWhat is the best answer?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Every check has gaps, so independent layers catch what any single one misses"},{"l":"B","t":"Regulations require at least three guardrail layers for every AI system"},{"l":"C","t":"A single check is always slower than several smaller ones in sequence"},{"l":"D","t":"Layered checks remove the need to monitor the agent in production"}],"a":["A"],"r":"No single guardrail is perfect. Independent layers (input screening, scoped permissions, hooks, approval gates, output checks and monitoring) mean one miss is less likely to cause harm.","x":["Correct. Defense in depth reduces the chance that one failure leads to harm.","Incorrect. No such universal requirement exists.","Incorrect. Layering is about coverage, not speed.","Incorrect. Monitoring is still needed."],"df":"easy","ob":"Runtime Guardrail Systems"},
{"id":"V291","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A research agent browses the web, reads the company's internal wiki, and can post messages to a public community forum on the company's behalf.\n\nWhich TWO changes most reduce its exfiltration risk? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Limit browsing to an allowlist of domains"},{"l":"B","t":"Remove or gate the public posting capability for sessions that have read internal content"},{"l":"C","t":"Increase the size of the model so that it is better at recognizing injected instructions"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask the model in its system prompt to treat all web content as potentially malicious"},{"l":"E","t":"Encrypt the internal wiki"}],"a":["A","B"],"r":"The high-risk combination is private data, untrusted input and an external channel. Removing or gating one leg, such as external posting after internal reads, or narrowing untrusted input sources, reduces the risk structurally.","x":["Correct. Fewer untrusted sources narrow the injection surface.","Correct. Breaking the combination of private data and external output removes the path.","Incorrect. Larger models can still be manipulated.","Incorrect. Prompt awareness does not stop a successful injection.","Incorrect. The agent still decrypts and reads the content."],"df":"hard","ob":"Threat Defense and Adversarial Testing for Agentic Systems"},
{"id":"V293","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team plans a red-team exercise for a tool-using customer-service agent before launch.\n\nWhich TWO elements make the exercise most useful? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Only generic jailbreak prompts copied from public lists, since they cover all known attacks"},{"l":"B","t":"A single session run by the developer who built the agent, to keep the exercise quick"},{"l":"C","t":"Clear success criteria and a process for turning each finding into a fix and a regression test"},{"l":"D","t":"Test only after launch"},{"l":"E","t":"Scenarios built on the agent's real tools and data"}],"a":["C","E"],"r":"Red-team exercises for agents should target the real attack surface (tools, data and channels), define what counts as success, and convert findings into fixes and regression tests.","x":["Incorrect. Generic prompts miss tool and data-specific paths.","Incorrect. The builder has blind spots about their own system.","Correct. Findings must lead to durable changes.","Incorrect. Pre-launch testing prevents harm to real users.","Correct. Testing the actual attack surface finds exploitable paths."],"df":"medium","ob":"Threat Defense and Adversarial Testing for Agentic Systems"},
{"id":"V296","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A security team maps its agent platform risks to the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.\n\nWhich function covers establishing policies, roles and accountability for AI risk across the organization?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Govern"},{"l":"B","t":"Map"},{"l":"C","t":"Manage"},{"l":"D","t":"Measure"}],"a":["A"],"r":"The NIST AI RMF has four functions: Govern (policies, roles, accountability and culture), Map (context and risk identification), Measure (assessment and tracking), and Manage (prioritizing and responding). Governance underpins the rest.","x":["Correct. Govern sets the policies, roles and accountability the other functions depend on.","Incorrect. Map establishes context and identifies risks for a system.","Incorrect. Manage prioritizes and acts on risks.","Incorrect. Measure assesses and tracks identified risks."],"df":"medium","ob":"Threat Defense and Adversarial Testing for Agentic Systems"},
{"id":"V298","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"An autonomous agent can create cloud resources to run experiments. Logs show it once launched 300 large instances in a loop after misreading an error message, costing $40,000 in a night.\n\nWhich OWASP LLM risk category best describes the underlying problem, and what fits as a mitigation?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Supply chain risk; audit the cloud provider's software for vulnerabilities"},{"l":"B","t":"Excessive agency; limit permissions, add quotas and approvals"},{"l":"C","t":"Prompt injection; filter the error messages so that they cannot contain instructions"},{"l":"D","t":"Sensitive information disclosure; encrypt the cloud credentials the agent uses"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Excessive agency covers agents with more functionality, permissions or autonomy than needed. Limits on permissions, quotas and approval gates for costly or irreversible actions bound the damage of unsafe autonomy, whatever triggers it.","x":["Incorrect. The problem is the agent's unchecked permissions.","Correct. The agent had more autonomy and capability than the task needed.","Incorrect. The agent misread an error; no attacker injected instructions.","Incorrect. Nothing was disclosed; resources were overused."],"df":"hard","ob":"Threat Defense and Adversarial Testing for Agentic Systems"},
{"id":"V302","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A developer asks why indirect prompt injection is a bigger concern for tool-using agents than for a chatbot without tools.\n\nWhat is the best answer?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Agents use easier-to-fool models"},{"l":"B","t":"Agents act, so injected text can become real actions"},{"l":"C","t":"Chatbots never read untrusted content, so they cannot be injected at all"},{"l":"D","t":"Agents have no system prompt, so they follow any instruction they see"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Tool-using agents read untrusted content and can act: send messages, change data, spend money. Injection then turns into actions with real consequences, which is why least privilege and deterministic controls matter.","x":["Incorrect. Model size is not the difference.","Correct. Tools turn manipulated text into consequential actions.","Incorrect. Chatbots can be injected too.","Incorrect. Agents have system prompts."],"df":"easy","ob":"Threat Defense and Adversarial Testing for Agentic Systems"},
{"id":"V303","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"A company's CI pipelines call the Claude API with a static API key stored as a secret in 140 repositories. A key leaked through a build log last quarter, and rotating it took two days of coordination.\n\nWhich change best addresses the underlying risk?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use workload identity federation: short-lived credentials, no stored key"},{"l":"B","t":"Encrypt the key in each repository with a team passphrase"},{"l":"C","t":"Store the key in a single shared repository that every pipeline clones before it runs its jobs"},{"l":"D","t":"Rotate the static key every week with a script that updates the secret in all 140 repositories"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Workload identity federation replaces static API keys with short-lived credentials issued to a workload's own identity, such as a CI job. There is no long-lived secret to leak, and access is scoped and revocable per workload.","x":["Correct. No long-lived secret exists to leak or rotate.","Incorrect. Encrypted copies still need a decryption secret.","Incorrect. One shared copy spreads access further.","Incorrect. Frequent rotation shortens exposure but keeps a leakable secret."],"df":"hard","ob":"Identity, Secrets, and Audit at Scale"},
{"id":"V306","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An agent has a CRM tool with a service account that can read and modify every customer record. The agent's job is to answer questions about the calling customer's own account.\n\nWhich TWO changes implement least-privilege permissioning? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep the broad account but instruct the agent to look only at the calling customer's records"},{"l":"B","t":"Grant read-only access, since answering questions requires no modifications"},{"l":"C","t":"Give the agent administrator access so that it never fails on permission errors"},{"l":"D","t":"Rotate the service account password monthly while keeping its current permissions"},{"l":"E","t":"Scope the tool to the authenticated customer's records, enforced by the backend, not the prompt"}],"a":["B","E"],"r":"Least privilege means the credential only allows what the task needs: read-only, scoped to the authenticated user's data, enforced in the backend. Prompt-based scoping and broad accounts leave the exposure in place.","x":["Incorrect. Prompt-based scoping can be bypassed.","Correct. Write access is unnecessary for the job.","Incorrect. Administrator access maximizes blast radius.","Incorrect. Rotation does not reduce what the account can do.","Correct. The agent cannot reach other customers' data."],"df":"hard","ob":"Identity, Secrets, and Audit at Scale"},
{"id":"V308","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A company rolls out Claude Code to engineering. Security wants no engineer to be able to use personal API keys or unapproved model providers through it.\n\nWhich TWO controls fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Configure authentication centrally in managed settings"},{"l":"B","t":"Have engineers sign an agreement"},{"l":"C","t":"Route traffic through an LLM gateway that holds the credentials and enforces approved providers"},{"l":"D","t":"Allow personal keys but review billing statements monthly to detect their use"},{"l":"E","t":"Distribute the organization's API key to every engineer so that they do not need personal keys"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Enterprise identity for coding agents is configured centrally: managed settings fix how Claude Code authenticates and where it sends traffic, and a gateway can hold credentials so engineers never see them.","x":["Correct. Central configuration controls which credentials and endpoints are used.","Incorrect. An agreement does not technically prevent it.","Correct. Engineers never handle provider keys directly.","Incorrect. Review after the fact does not prevent it.","Incorrect. Distributing a shared key multiplies exposure."],"df":"medium","ob":"Identity, Secrets, and Audit at Scale"},
{"id":"V313","s":1,"d":7,"ty":"single","q":"An organization's audit log for Claude Code shows that hooks and permission rules were bypassed on several machines. Investigation finds engineers launched sessions with flags that loaded local settings overriding the team's rules.\n\nWhich control prevents this?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Use managed settings that allow only managed rules and hooks"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask engineers in the onboarding guide not to override the team's settings"},{"l":"C","t":"Remove hooks entirely, since they can be bypassed and give a false sense of security"},{"l":"D","t":"Check each engineer's settings files once a month and correct any overrides"}],"a":["A"],"r":"Managed settings can restrict permission rules and hooks to those the organization deploys and block local overrides, so policies hold on every machine. Guidance and periodic checks do not stop bypasses.","x":["Correct. Managed-only settings cannot be overridden by local configuration.","Incorrect. Guidance does not enforce the rule.","Incorrect. Removing controls weakens security further.","Incorrect. A monthly check leaves weeks of bypass."],"df":"hard","ob":"Identity, Secrets, and Audit at Scale"},
{"id":"V316","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"An engineer splits a large refactor into six tasks and runs six Claude Code sessions in parallel in the same checkout. Sessions overwrite each other's edits, and test results are inconsistent because files change mid-run.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Give all six sessions the same CLAUDE.md so that they follow identical conventions while editing"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask each session in its prompt to avoid editing files that other sessions might be working on"},{"l":"C","t":"Run each session in its own Git worktree on its own branch, and merge the branches afterward"},{"l":"D","t":"Run the six sessions one after another in the same checkout so that edits never overlap at all"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Parallel sessions need isolation. A worktree gives each session its own checkout and branch, so edits and test runs do not interfere, and the work is merged afterward through normal review.","x":["Incorrect. Shared conventions do not stop file collisions.","Incorrect. Sessions cannot see each other's work, so they cannot coordinate.","Correct. Separate working trees stop sessions colliding on files.","Incorrect. Serial runs avoid collisions but give up the parallelism."],"df":"hard","ob":"Parallel Agent Orchestration for Engineering Work"},
{"id":"V320","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"A team runs parallel agents on worktrees. Each worktree needs a local environment file that is not committed to Git, and sessions fail at startup because it is missing.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run all sessions in the main checkout instead of in separate worktrees"},{"l":"B","t":"List the file in .worktreeinclude so that it is copied into each new worktree"},{"l":"C","t":"Commit the environment file to the repository so that every worktree has it"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask each session in its prompt to create the environment file from memory"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Worktrees start from the repository, so ignored files are missing. A .worktreeinclude file lists git-ignored files to copy into each worktree, keeping secrets out of version control.","x":["Incorrect. That brings back file collisions.","Correct. Ignored files the sessions need are copied automatically.","Incorrect. Committing it exposes local secrets.","Incorrect. The session cannot know the file's contents."],"df":"medium","ob":"Parallel Agent Orchestration for Engineering Work"},
{"id":"V322","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"Two parallel agents produced branches that both modified the same configuration file in different ways. The merge has conflicts.\n\nWhat is the best approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Keep whichever branch merged first"},{"l":"B","t":"Resolve it in one session aware of both intents, then test"},{"l":"C","t":"Merge both branches without resolving, since the CI will reveal any problems later"},{"l":"D","t":"Ask both agents to re-run their tasks until one of them avoids touching the file"}],"a":["B"],"r":"Merge conflicts from parallel work need resolution with both intents in view, followed by verification. Planning tasks to avoid shared files reduces how often this happens.","x":["Incorrect. Discarding work may break the second feature.","Correct. One context can reconcile both intents and verify the result.","Incorrect. Unresolved conflicts do not merge.","Incorrect. Re-running does not resolve the conflicting requirements."],"df":"medium","ob":"Parallel Agent Orchestration for Engineering Work"},
{"id":"V323","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A platform team wants fleet-level telemetry for its parallel coding agents.\n\nWhich TWO metrics are most useful for spotting fleet-level failure modes? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"The average length of each agent's commit message"},{"l":"B","t":"CI failures grouped by test and base commit"},{"l":"C","t":"The number of agents running at the same moment"},{"l":"D","t":"Rework rate after review, by task type"},{"l":"E","t":"The total number of files read by all agents each day"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Fleet telemetry should expose shared failure causes and outcome quality: CI failures grouped by cause and starting point, and rework rates by task type.","x":["Incorrect. Message length does not indicate quality.","Correct. Grouping reveals shared causes across agents.","Incorrect. Concurrency alone does not show failures.","Correct. It shows which kinds of task the fleet handles poorly.","Incorrect. File reads measure activity, not outcomes."],"df":"medium","ob":"Parallel Agent Orchestration for Engineering Work"},
{"id":"V325","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"A team wants to introduce AI code review on all pull requests and eventually let its findings block merges. Engineers worry about false positives slowing work.\n\nWhat rollout is most appropriate?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Run it in shadow mode against human reviews, and gate merges once precision is acceptable"},{"l":"B","t":"Replace human review entirely with AI review, since AI review is faster and more consistent across pull requests"},{"l":"C","t":"Make the AI review a required check from the first day, so that every pull request benefits from it immediately"},{"l":"D","t":"Let each engineer decide whether to read the AI findings, and never measure their accuracy"}],"a":["A"],"r":"AI code review should start in shadow mode, with findings compared against human reviews to measure precision and recall. Only once it is calibrated should it gain merge-gating authority, with humans still approving.","x":["Correct. Calibration against human review comes before gating authority.","Incorrect. Human oversight remains necessary for intent and risk.","Incorrect. An uncalibrated gate blocks merges on false positives.","Incorrect. Unmeasured findings cannot earn gating authority."],"df":"hard","ob":"Verification and AI Code Review Operations"},
{"id":"V328","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"multi","se":3,"q":"A team runs coding agents unattended overnight and reviews their output each morning.\n\nWhich THREE verification practices fit unsupervised runs? **(Select THREE.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Have a fresh session, with no memory of the work, review it"},{"l":"B","t":"Trust the agent's summary when it says tests passed, to save review time"},{"l":"C","t":"Start review from the diff, not the agent's summary of what it did"},{"l":"D","t":"Run unattended jobs in bypass permissions mode so that nothing blocks them overnight"},{"l":"E","t":"Skip review for changes under 50 lines, since small changes are low risk"},{"l":"F","t":"Gate the run's completion on tests through a Stop hook, and check the run's exit status"}],"a":["A","C","F"],"r":"Verify in proportion to how little the run was watched: read the diff first, make tests a hook-enforced gate with the exit status checked, and get a cold second review on anything that matters.","x":["Correct. A fresh context catches what the writer talked itself past.","Incorrect. Claims of passing tests need evidence.","Correct. A tidy summary can hide changes to unexpected files.","Incorrect. Bypass mode removes the safety net unattended runs need.","Incorrect. Small changes can break critical paths.","Correct. Tests become a gate the agent cannot skip."],"df":"hard","ob":"Verification and AI Code Review Operations"},
{"id":"V329","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"AI code review on a team's repositories produces many style comments, and engineers have started ignoring all of its findings, including real bugs.\n\nWhat should the tech lead do?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Reserve high severity for real defects and cap nits"},{"l":"B","t":"Turn off the AI review"},{"l":"C","t":"Increase the review's model tier so that it finds more issues on each pull request"},{"l":"D","t":"Require engineers to reply to every AI comment before they can merge"}],"a":["A"],"r":"AI review must be calibrated for signal: a review policy that defines severity, caps nits and excludes what CI already enforces keeps findings worth reading.","x":["Correct. Fewer, better findings restore attention to what matters.","Incorrect. That loses the real bugs it finds.","Incorrect. More findings add to the noise.","Incorrect. Forced replies to noise increase fatigue."],"df":"medium","ob":"Verification and AI Code Review Operations"},
{"id":"V330","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"A team measures its AI code review in shadow mode over six weeks, comparing it with human reviewers' findings.\n\nWhich TWO measures should decide whether it can gate merges? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Recall on serious defects: the share of human-found serious issues it also found"},{"l":"B","t":"How quickly it posts its review after each pull request opens"},{"l":"C","t":"Precision: the share of its blocking-level findings that humans confirm as real"},{"l":"D","t":"The number of comments it posts per pull request on average"},{"l":"E","t":"Engineers' opinions about whether the comments are helpful, without checking them"}],"a":["A","C"],"r":"Calibrating AI review against human review means measuring precision of the findings that would block, and recall on serious defects. Those decide whether it can gate merges and where humans must still look.","x":["Correct. Gaps show where human review is still required.","Incorrect. Speed matters less than accuracy for gating.","Correct. Low precision means blocking good changes.","Incorrect. Volume says nothing about accuracy.","Incorrect. Opinions without verification are weak evidence."],"df":"hard","ob":"Verification and AI Code Review Operations"},
{"id":"V331","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"A team gives Claude a way to check its own work before an engineer reviews it. Their web app has no automated UI tests.\n\nWhat is the most useful verification loop for UI changes?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask Claude to describe how the UI will look after its change"},{"l":"B","t":"Give Claude a screenshot tool and the mock to compare against"},{"l":"C","t":"Rely on the engineer to check every UI change by hand after the agent finishes"},{"l":"D","t":"Have Claude count the lines of CSS it changed and stay under a limit"}],"a":["B"],"r":"A feedback loop gives the agent a way to verify its own work. For UI work, a browser or screenshot tool plus a reference mock lets it implement, compare and adjust before a person reviews.","x":["Incorrect. A description is not a check.","Correct. A visual check lets the agent see whether the UI matches.","Incorrect. That keeps the engineer as the bottleneck.","Incorrect. Line counts do not verify appearance."],"df":"medium","ob":"Verification and AI Code Review Operations"},
{"id":"V334","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"A CI job runs Claude Code non-interactively to fix lint errors. It sometimes hangs until the job times out, because Claude is waiting for permission to run a command.\n\nWhat is the best fix?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Increase the CI job's timeout so that the permission prompt eventually times out by itself"},{"l":"B","t":"Use bypass permissions mode so that every command runs without any approval or checks"},{"l":"C","t":"Use a mode that denies anything not pre-approved, and list the tools"},{"l":"D","t":"Have an engineer watch the CI logs and approve prompts by hand when they appear"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Unattended runs need a permission mode that never waits for input: pre-approve the tools the job needs and deny the rest, so the job fails fast instead of hanging. Bypass mode removes the safety net.","x":["Incorrect. Waiting longer does not answer the prompt.","Incorrect. Bypass mode removes all checks and belongs only in isolated sandboxes.","Correct. Calls that would prompt are refused instead of waiting.","Incorrect. Unattended runs cannot depend on someone watching."],"df":"hard","ob":"Unattended and Event-Driven Agent Operations"},
{"id":"V344","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An organization wants teams to share Skills, subagents and hooks without copying files between repositories, while controlling where plugins come from.\n\nWhich TWO mechanisms fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Ask every team to rebuild the same Skills independently from a written description"},{"l":"B","t":"Email zip files of each team's .claude directory to other teams when they ask"},{"l":"C","t":"Restrict plugin installation to approved marketplaces through managed settings"},{"l":"D","t":"Package shared configuration as plugins in an internal marketplace with versions"},{"l":"E","t":"Allow any public marketplace, since listed plugins have been reviewed by Anthropic"}],"a":["C","D"],"r":"Plugins package Skills, subagents, hooks and MCP configuration as versioned, installable units, and managed settings can restrict installation to approved marketplaces, giving reuse with governance.","x":["Incorrect. Rebuilding duplicates effort and creates inconsistency.","Incorrect. Copies drift and bypass review.","Correct. Only vetted sources can be installed.","Correct. One install brings a versioned, reviewed setup.","Incorrect. Listing is not the same as being trusted."],"df":"medium","ob":"Organization-Wide Coding Agent Configuration"},
{"id":"V345","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"A platform team wants usage, cost and tool-decision telemetry from every engineer's Claude Code sessions in the company's observability stack.\n\nWhat is the right configuration approach?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Rely on the monthly invoice for usage data, since it lists cost by user"},{"l":"B","t":"Ask each engineer to turn on telemetry in their personal settings during onboarding"},{"l":"C","t":"Enable OpenTelemetry export through managed settings, pointing at the company's collector"},{"l":"D","t":"Collect each engineer's local log files with a script that runs at the end of every day"}],"a":["C"],"r":"Organization-wide telemetry is configured centrally: managed settings enable OpenTelemetry export to the company's collector for every session, with content logging controlled separately.","x":["Incorrect. An invoice lacks session-level and tool data.","Incorrect. Opt-in by individuals leaves gaps.","Correct. Central settings ensure every session reports to the same place.","Incorrect. Scraping local files is unreliable and incomplete."],"df":"medium","ob":"Organization-Wide Coding Agent Configuration"},
{"id":"V349","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"single","q":"An organization wants only approved MCP servers to be usable from Claude Code across the company.\n\nWhere should this be enforced?","o":[{"l":"A","t":"In a wiki page listing approved servers for engineers to check"},{"l":"B","t":"In each engineer's user settings during onboarding"},{"l":"C","t":"In each repository's .mcp.json file, listing the approved servers"},{"l":"D","t":"In managed settings that allow only managed MCP servers"}],"a":["D"],"r":"Managed settings can make the agent's MCP tool surface an allowlist owned by the platform team, so only approved servers load in any session.","x":["Incorrect. A list does not block other servers.","Incorrect. User settings can be edited afterward.","Incorrect. Project files can be changed or bypassed.","Correct. Central enforcement applies to every session."],"df":"medium","ob":"Organization-Wide Coding Agent Configuration"},
{"id":"V350","s":1,"d":8,"ty":"multi","se":2,"q":"An organization is setting default models and effort levels for Claude Code across teams to balance cost and capability.\n\nWhich TWO practices fit? **(Select TWO.)**","o":[{"l":"A","t":"Require the smallest model for all tasks"},{"l":"B","t":"Review usage and cost data regularly and adjust defaults when evidence shows a better balance"},{"l":"C","t":"Change defaults daily so that engineers try every combination of model and effort"},{"l":"D","t":"Set default model and effort centrally, with per-role overrides"},{"l":"E","t":"Let every engineer choose any setting with no defaults, since engineers know their work best"}],"a":["B","D"],"r":"Organization-wide configuration sets defaults that fit most work, allows overrides for roles that need more, and adjusts based on usage data.","x":["Incorrect. The smallest model fails harder tasks.","Correct. Defaults should follow measured usage.","Incorrect. Constant changes make results hard to compare.","Correct. Defaults drive most usage, and roles cover exceptions.","Incorrect. Without defaults, the most expensive settings often become the norm."],"df":"medium","ob":"Organization-Wide Coding Agent Configuration"}
];
